{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'cat pose-mcp/internal/pose/closeout_adr_associations.go'","aggregated_output":"package pose\n\n// ADR associations of a roadmap, for the when-justified roadmap rule of the\n// closeout review evaluator (spec pose-closeout-review-policy-by-cost-benefit).\n//\n// An association is explicit, never inferred from prose:\n//   - forward: the roadmap's `adrs: [adr:<file>.md]` frontmatter, or a local\n//     Markdown link from its body to a file in .pose/adr/;\n//   - backward: an ADR's `roadmaps: [roadmap:<slug>]` (or the qualified\n//     `xref:<project>/roadmap:<slug>`) frontmatter, or a local Markdown link\n//     from the ADR to .pose/roadmaps/<slug>.md.\n//\n// Only the owning project's .pose/adr/ is inventoried; nothing searches other\n// projects. A malformed declaration, a link to a missing file or an\n// incomplete inventory is unknown, which can never prove absence.\n\nimport (\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"github.com/yuin/goldmark\"\n\t\"github.com/yuin/goldmark/ast\"\n\t\"github.com/yuin/goldmark/text\"\n\t\"html\"\n\t\"net/url\"\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"path/filepath\"\n\t\"regexp\"\n\t\"sort\"\n\t\"strings\"\n)\n\nconst (\n\tcloseoutADRInventoryLimit = 4096\n\tcloseoutADRSizeLimit      = 1 << 20\n)\n\nfunc (s Store) adrDir() string { return filepath.Join(s.Root, \".pose\", \"adr\") }\n\n// markdownLinkDestinations lists the local link destinations of a Markdown\n// body as CommonMark reads them (ADR\n// governance-association-links-are-read-with-a-commonmark-parser): the links\n// and images of its syntax tree, reference definitions resolved, code and\n// comments excluded. Each destination is decoded in CommonMark precedence,\n// cut at its fragment and query, and percent-decoded. The second list holds\n// what the reader cannot speak for: a destination whose escapes do not\n// decode, and raw HTML carrying a link attribute.\nfunc markdownLinkDestinations(body string) ([]string, []string) {\n\tout, undecodable := []string{}, []string{}\n\tsource := []byte(body)\n\tdoc := goldmark.New().Parser().Parse(text.NewReader(source))\n\tadd := func(raw string) {\n\t\tdest := decodeMarkdownDestination(raw)\n\t\tif i := strings.IndexAny(dest, \"#?\"); i >= 0 {\n\t\t\tdest = dest[:i]\n\t\t}\n\t\tdecoded, err := url.PathUnescape(dest)\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\tundecodable = append(undecodable, raw)\n\t\t\treturn\n\t\t}\n\t\tdest = strings.TrimSpace(decoded)\n\t\tif dest == \"\" || strings.Contains(dest, \"://\") || strings.HasPrefix(dest, \"mailto:\") || strings.HasPrefix(dest, \"/\") {\n\t\t\treturn\n\t\t}\n\t\tout = append(out, dest)\n\t}\n\thtmlLink := func(raw string) {\n\t\tif markdownHTMLLinkAttributeRE.MatchString(raw) {\n\t\t\tundecodable = append(undecodable, \"raw HTML \"+strings.TrimSpace(raw))\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\t_ = ast.Walk(doc, func(node ast.Node, entering bool) (ast.WalkStatus, error) {\n\t\tif !entering {\n\t\t\treturn ast.WalkContinue, nil\n\t\t}\n\t\tswitch n := node.(type) {\n\t\tcase *ast.Link:\n\t\t\tadd(string(n.Destination))\n\t\tcase *ast.Image:\n\t\t\tadd(string(n.Destination))\n\t\tcase *ast.RawHTML:\n\t\t\tvar raw strings.Builder\n\t\t\tfor i := 0; i < n.Segments.Len(); i++ {\n\t\t\t\tsegment := n.Segments.At(i)\n\t\t\t\traw.Write(segment.Value(source))\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\thtmlLink(raw.String())\n\t\tcase *ast.HTMLBlock:\n\t\t\tvar raw strings.Builder\n\t\t\tfor i := 0; i < n.Lines().Len(); i++ {\n\t\t\t\tline := n.Lines().At(i)\n\t\t\t\traw.Write(line.Value(source))\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif n.HasClosure() {\n\t\t\t\traw.Write(n.ClosureLine.Value(source))\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\thtmlLink(raw.String())\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn ast.WalkContinue, nil\n\t})\n\treturn out, undecodable\n}\n\nvar (\n\t// markdownEntityRE is a CommonMark character reference at the start of a\n\t// string: decimal, hexadecimal or named.\n\tmarkdownEntityRE = regexp.MustCompile(`^&(#[0-9]{1,7}|#[xX][0-9a-fA-F]{1,6}|[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9]{1,31});`)\n\t// markdownHTMLLinkAttributeRE finds an attribute through which raw HTML\n\t// makes a link.\n\tmarkdownHTMLLinkAttributeRE = regexp.MustCompile(`(?i)\\b(href|src)\\s*=`)\n)\n\n// decodeMarkdownDestination decodes a raw link destination in one\n// left-to-right pass, as CommonMark does: a backslash makes the next ASCII\n// punctuation literal, and only an unescaped & starts a character reference.\nfunc decodeMarkdownDestination(raw string) string {\n\tvar b strings.Builder\n\tfor i := 0; i < len(raw); {\n\t\tc := raw[i]\n\t\tif c == '\\\\' && i+1 < len(raw) && isASCIIPunctuation(raw[i+1]) {\n\t\t\tb.WriteByte(raw[i+1])\n\t\t\ti += 2\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tif c == '&' {\n\t\t\tif entity := markdownEntityRE.FindString(raw[i:]); entity != \"\" {\n\t\t\t\tif decoded := html.UnescapeString(entity); decoded != entity {\n\t\t\t\t\tb.WriteString(decoded)\n\t\t\t\t\ti += len(entity)\n\t\t\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t\tb.WriteByte(c)\n\t\ti++\n\t}\n\treturn b.String()\n}\n\nfunc isASCIIPunctuation(c byte) bool {\n\treturn c >= '!' && c <= '/' || c >= ':' && c <= '@' || c >= '[' && c <= '`' || c >= '{' && c <= '~'\n}\n\n// associationList reads one association key (adrs: on a roadmap, roadmaps: on\n// an ADR) from the raw frontmatter of a governance file, strictly: a key\n// declared more than once, declared with no value, outside a [list], or with\n// an empty item is reported, never read as an absent or empty association. An\n// explicit [] declares none (review round 12, N39).\nfunc associationList(raw, key, owner string) ([]string, bool, string) {\n\tlines := strings.Split(strings.ReplaceAll(raw, \"\\r\\n\", \"\\n\"), \"\\n\")\n\tif len(lines) == 0 || strings.TrimSpace(lines[0]) != \"---\" {\n\t\treturn nil, false, \"\"\n\t}\n\tvalues := []string{}\n\tfor _, line := range lines[1:] {\n\t\tif strings.TrimSpace(line) == \"---\" {\n\t\t\tbreak\n\t\t}\n\t\t// The key and value as SplitFrontmatter reads them: the key trimmed,\n\t\t// a template comment removed (review round 13, N45).\n\t\tif name, value, ok := strings.Cut(line, \":\"); ok && strings.TrimSpace(name) == key {\n\t\t\tvalues = append(values, cleanValue(value))\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tswitch {\n\tcase len(values) == 0:\n\t\treturn nil, false, \"\"\n\tcase len(values) > 1:\n\t\treturn nil, true, fmt.Sprintf(\"%s declares %s: %d times; one declaration is required\", owner, key, len(values))\n\t}\n\tvalue := values[0]\n\tif value == \"\" {\n\t\treturn nil, true, fmt.Sprintf(\"%s declares %s: with no value; declare [] for none\", owner, key)\n\t}\n\tif !strings.HasPrefix(value, \"[\") || !strings.HasSuffix(value, \"]\") {\n\t\treturn nil, true, fmt.Sprintf(\"%s declares %s: outside a [list]\", owner, key)\n\t}\n\tinner := strings.TrimSpace(value[1 : len(value)-1])\n\tif inner == \"\" {\n\t\treturn []string{}, true, \"\"\n\t}\n\titems := []string{}\n\tfor _, item := range strings.Split(inner, \",\") {\n\t\titem = strings.TrimSpace(item)\n\t\tif item == \"\" {\n\t\t\treturn nil, true, fmt.Sprintf(\"%s declares %s: with an empty item\", owner, key)\n\t\t}\n\t\titems = append(items, item)\n\t}\n\treturn items, true, \"\"\n}\n\n// validADRBasename accepts exactly a file name ending in .md directly inside\n// .pose/adr/: no directory, traversal, hidden file or URI.\nfunc validADRBasename(name string) bool {\n\treturn name != \"\" && filepath.Base(name) == name && !strings.ContainsAny(name, `/\\:`) &&\n\t\t!strings.HasPrefix(name, \".\") && strings.HasSuffix(name, \".md\") && name != \".md\"\n}\n\n// regularADR reports a regular, non-symlinked file of .pose/adr/ within the root.\nfunc (s Store) regularADR(name string) error {\n\tpath := filepath.Join(s.adrDir(), name)\n\tinfo, err := s.fsys().Lstat(path)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\tif info.Mode()&os.ModeSymlink != 0 || !info.Mode().IsRegular() || !artifactPathWithin(s.fsys(), s.Root, path) {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"not a regular file inside .pose/adr\")\n\t}\n\treturn nil\n}\n\n// closeoutADRAssociations returns the sorted ADR basenames associated with a\n// roadmap, and the diagnostics that make the answer unknown.\nfunc (s Store) closeoutADRAssociations(rm *Roadmap, project string) ([]string, []string) {\n\tfound := map[string]bool{}\n\tdiagnostics := []string{}\n\trawRoadmap, readErr := s.fsys().ReadFile(rm.Path)\n\tif readErr != nil {\n\t\tdiagnostics = append(diagnostics, \"roadmap \"+rm.Slug+\" could not be read for its adrs: \"+readErr.Error())\n\t}\n\tdeclared, _, declarationProblem := associationList(string(rawRoadmap), \"adrs\", \"roadmap \"+rm.Slug)\n\tif declarationProblem != \"\" {\n\t\tdiagnostics = append(diagnostics, declarationProblem)\n\t}\n\tfor _, item := range declared {\n\t\tname, ok := strings.CutPrefix(item, \"adr:\")\n\t\tif !ok || !validADRBasename(name) {\n\t\t\tdiagnostics = append(diagnostics, fmt.Sprintf(\"roadmap %s declares %q, not adr:<file>.md\", rm.Slug, item))\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tif err := s.regularADR(name); err != nil {\n\t\t\tdiagnostics = append(diagnostics, fmt.Sprintf(\"roadmap %s declares adr:%s, which cannot be read: %v\", rm.Slug, name, err))\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tfound[name] = true\n\t}\n\tbody := rm.Body\n\tif body == \"\" {\n\t\tif full, err := s.GetRoadmap(rm.Slug); err == nil {\n\t\t\tbody = full.Body\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\troadmapDir := filepath.Dir(rm.Path)\n\tlinks, undecodable := markdownLinkDestinations(body)\n\tfor _, dest := range undecodable {\n\t\tdiagnostics = append(diagnostics, fmt.Sprintf(\"roadmap %s links to %s, which the association reader cannot resolve (an undecodable escape or raw HTML link)\", rm.Slug, dest))\n\t}\n\tfor _, dest := range links {\n\t\ttarget := filepath.Clean(filepath.Join(roadmapDir, filepath.FromSlash(dest)))\n\t\tif filepath.Dir(target) != filepath.Clean(s.adrDir()) || !strings.HasSuffix(target, \".md\") {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tname := filepath.Base(target)\n\t\tif err := s.regularADR(name); err != nil {\n\t\t\tdiagnostics = append(diagnostics, fmt.Sprintf(\"roadmap %s links to %s, which cannot be read: %v\", rm.Slug, dest, err))\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tfound[name] = true\n\t}\n\tentries, err := s.fsys().ReadDir(s.adrDir())\n\tif err != nil && !os.IsNotExist(err) {\n\t\tdiagnostics = append(diagnostics, \"the ADR inventory could not be read: \"+err.Error())\n\t}\n\tif len(entries) > closeoutADRInventoryLimit {\n\t\tdiagnostics = append(diagnostics, fmt.Sprintf(\"the ADR inventory has %d entries, above the limit of %d\", len(entries), closeoutADRInventoryLimit))\n\t\tentries = nil\n\t}\n\troadmapPath := filepath.Clean(filepath.Join(s.roadmapsDir(), rm.Slug+\".md\"))\n\tfor _, entry := range entries {\n\t\tname := entry.Name()\n\t\t// Every candidate is read, the associated ones included: its size,\n\t\t// its readability and its own declaration are inputs. One that\n\t\t// cannot be read could hold a backlink, so it is unknown.\n\t\tif entry.IsDir() || !strings.HasSuffix(name, \".md\") {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tif err := s.regularADR(name); err != nil {\n\t\t\tdiagnostics = append(diagnostics, \"ADR \"+name+\" is not a regular file of .pose/adr: \"+err.Error())\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tpath := filepath.Join(s.adrDir(), name)\n\t\tif info, err := s.fsys().Lstat(path); err != nil || info.Size() > closeoutADRSizeLimit {\n\t\t\tdiagnostics = append(diagnostics, \"ADR \"+name+\" is unreadable or above the size limit\")\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\traw, err := s.fsys().ReadFile(path)\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\tdiagnostics = append(diagnostics, \"ADR \"+name+\" could not be read: \"+err.Error())\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\t_, adrBody := SplitFrontmatter(string(raw))\n\t\tbackward, _, backwardProblem := associationList(string(raw), \"roadmaps\", \"ADR \"+name)\n\t\tif backwardProblem != \"\" {\n\t\t\tdiagnostics = append(diagnostics, backwardProblem)\n\t\t}\n\t\tfor _, item := range backward {\n\t\t\tref, err := ParseArtifactRef(item)\n\t\t\tif err != nil || ref.Kind != \"roadmap\" || ref.Milestone != \"\" {\n\t\t\t\tdiagnostics = append(diagnostics, fmt.Sprintf(\"ADR %s declares %q, not roadmap:<slug>\", name, item))\n\t\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif ref.Project != \"\" && ref.Project != project {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue // a roadmap of another project: not this association\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif _, err := s.GetRoadmap(ref.Slug); err != nil {\n\t\t\t\tdiagnostics = append(diagnostics, fmt.Sprintf(\"ADR %s declares %s, which names no roadmap of this project\", name, item))\n\t\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif ref.Slug == rm.Slug {\n\t\t\t\tfound[name] = true\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t\tbacklinks, undecodableBacklinks := markdownLinkDestinations(adrBody)\n\t\tfor _, dest := range undecodableBacklinks {\n\t\t\tdiagnostics = append(diagnostics, \"ADR \"+name+\" links to \"+dest+\", which the association reader cannot resolve (an undecodable escape or raw HTML link)\")\n\t\t}\n\t\tfor _, dest := range backlinks {\n\t\t\ttarget := filepath.Clean(filepath.Join(s.adrDir(), filepath.FromSlash(dest)))\n\t\t\tif filepath.Dir(target) != filepath.Clean(s.roadmapsDir()) || !strings.HasSuffix(target, \".md\") {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t// A link into the roadmaps namespace is an association input: one\n\t\t\t// that does not resolve to a roadmap leaves the answer unknown.\n\t\t\tif info, err := s.fsys().Lstat(target); err != nil || !info.Mode().IsRegular() || !artifactPathWithin(s.fsys(), s.Root, target) {\n\t\t\t\tdiagnostics = append(diagnostics, \"ADR \"+name+\" links to \"+dest+\", which is not a roadmap of this project\")\n\t\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t// The target is read as the roadmap it names, as a roadmaps:\n\t\t\t// declaration is: a regular entry that cannot be read or parsed\n\t\t\t// leaves the answer unknown, never an absence. The roadmaps README\n\t\t\t// is no roadmap, as ListRoadmaps reads the namespace.\n\t\t\tif strings.EqualFold(filepath.Base(target), \"README.md\") {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif _, err := s.GetRoadmap(strings.TrimSuffix(filepath.Base(target), \".md\")); err != nil {\n\t\t\t\tdiagnostics = append(diagnostics, \"ADR \"+name+\" links to \"+dest+\", which could not be read as a roadmap of this project\")\n\t\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif target == roadmapPath {\n\t\t\t\tfound[name] = true\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tadrs := make([]string, 0, len(found))\n\tfor name := range found {\n\t\tadrs = append(adrs, name)\n\t}\n\tsort.Strings(adrs)\n\tsort.Strings(diagnostics)\n\treturn adrs, diagnostics\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'cat .pose/adr/2026-11-14-governance-association-links-are-read-with-a-commonmark-parser.md .pose/adr/2026-11-12-closeout-review-policy-by-cost-and-benefit.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'cat .pose/adr/2026-11-14-governance-association-links-are-read-with-a-commonmark-parser.md .pose/adr/2026-11-12-closeout-review-policy-by-cost-and-benefit.md'","aggregated_output":"# ADR: Governance association links are read with a CommonMark parser\n\n## Status\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nSpec `pose-closeout-review-policy-by-cost-benefit` (R3) makes a roadmap's\ncloseout review depend on its ADR associations: `adrs:` on the roadmap,\n`roadmaps:` on the ADR, or a local Markdown link either way. Under\n`when-justified` a roadmap with more than one canonical spec and no\nassociation is exempt, so a real link the reader misses grants an exemption\nthe policy does not allow.\n\nThe links were read by a hand-written extractor: two regular expressions for\ninline and reference links, then ad hoc decoding. Review rounds 12 to 16 of\nthat spec each found valid CommonMark in which a real association vanished\nwithout a diagnostic and an exemption followed:\n\n- a filter for code and comments truncated fence delimiters, ignored indented\n  code and let a comment opener inside code swallow a later link (N44, N46);\n- a destination with balanced parentheses was cut short (N47);\n- percent escapes, character references and backslash escapes were not\n  decoded, or decoded in the wrong order (N48, N52);\n- a reference definition spanning two lines, inside a block quote, or with an\n  escaped bracket in its label was not seen (N50);\n- an escaped `>` inside an angle-bracket destination ended it early (N51).\n\nEach fix opened the next variant. The extractor approximates a grammar that\nis defined by a specification, and every approximation is a place where an\nexemption can be manufactured.\n\n## Decision\n\nRead the structure of an association-bearing document (a roadmap body, an\nADR body) with a CommonMark parser, `github.com/yuin/goldmark`, and take the\nassociation links from its syntax tree: every link and image node, with\nreference definitions resolved, block quotes and multi-line labels handled,\nand code spans, code blocks and comments excluded as CommonMark excludes\nthem.\n\nDecode each destination in POSE, in CommonMark precedence: one left-to-right\npass in which a backslash escape makes the next ASCII punctuation literal and\nan unescaped `&` starts a character reference; then cut the fragment and the\nquery; then decode percent escapes. goldmark's own HTML renderer applies\nbackslash escapes before character references, which turns `\\&num;` into `#`;\nthe association reader does not use the renderer and does not inherit that\norder.\n\nFail closed where the parser cannot speak for the rendered document: raw HTML\n(inline or block) carrying an `href` or `src` attribute leaves the answer\nunknown, as does a destination whose percent escapes do not decode. A\ndocument the parser reads normally is not otherwise second-guessed: a link\nshown inside code is not an association, and a destination with balanced\nparentheses is read whole.\n\n## Alternatives rejected\n\n- **Keep fixing the extractor.** Five rounds showed the variants do not\n  converge; the extractor would have to become a CommonMark parser to stop.\n- **Fail closed on any escape or entity character.** It stops the silent\n  drops but makes ordinary prose and examples require review, and still\n  misses structural forms such as reference definitions in block quotes.\n- **A C CommonMark library through cgo.** The engine is a static, pure Go\n  distribution; cgo would change how it is built and released.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- The engine gains one dependency, `github.com/yuin/goldmark` v1.8.4: MIT\n  licensed (compatible with the distribution's Apache-2.0), pure Go, no\n  transitive module dependencies, maintained, and clean under `govulncheck`\n  when adopted. It is used only to parse; nothing is rendered with it.\n- The association reader follows CommonMark, not GitHub Flavored Markdown\n  extensions: an extension that creates links from text (autolinked bare\n  paths) is not an association, as a bare path in prose never was.\n- Follow-up F6 of the closeout review policy spec (links shown as code\n  counting as associations) is resolved by this decision.\n- A future change of the parser version is a change of the association\n  contract and is reviewed as one.\n# ADR: Closeout review policy by cost and benefit\n\n## Status\nAccepted — the maintainer approved this base explicitly on 2026-11-12, including\nlegacy compatibility and `always` requiring a one-member milestone's own review.\nThis records the architectural decision, not policy adoption or a delivery attestation.\n\n## Context\n\nSpec [pose-closeout-review-policy-by-cost-benefit](../specs/2026-11-11-pose-closeout-review-policy-by-cost-benefit.md)\nseparates the mandatory close of an aggregate scope from its independent review.\nThe current [one-member milestone rule](2026-11-02-a-milestone-of-one-member-is-that-member.md)\ninherits the member's approval, while multi-member milestones and roadmaps normally\nrequire their own reviews. Review value depends on composition; the maintainer\nchose explicit deterministic thresholds instead of a judgment made by an agent.\n\nSealed bundles include policy and review plans. A new setting must not reinterpret\nthe historical contract, erase a rejection, skip member reviews, or infer adoption\nfrom an unrecognized optional field.\n\n## Decision\n\nStore `milestone_review_required` and `roadmap_review_required` in the versioned\ninstance review policy `.pose/policy/review.json`. Accept exactly `never`,\n`when-justified`, and `always`; reject unknown values before preparing a current\nplan or writing a closeout. Use one domain evaluator across CLI, MCP, planning,\nverification, state, closeout and federation; expose its reason in their projections.\n\n- `never`: require no independent review of that aggregate scope.\n- `always`: require the scope's own independent review, including a one-member\n  milestone. An inherited member approval does not satisfy this explicit choice.\n- `when-justified`, milestone: require review exactly when it has more than two\n  distinct canonical spec members. Duplicate aliases do not increase the count.\n- `when-justified`, roadmap: require review exactly when its union of milestones\n  contains more than one distinct canonical spec and at least one resolved ADR\n  association. Count the spec once even if it appears in multiple milestones.\n\nRecognize an ADR association declared by the roadmap or a canonical association\nback from an ADR. Resolve explicit references, not incidental words in prose.\nUnknown, unreadable or ambiguous member/association inputs cannot yield an\nexemption: report the missing knowledge and refuse the close until resolved.\nAcceptance tests must fix the reference syntax in the implementation spec before\nchanging its parser.\n\nThe defaults for newly installed/adopting instances are `never` for both keys.\nIntroduce a review-policy schema version that older engines reject. A legacy\npolicy with neither key retains its legacy semantics until explicit adoption;\nsetup/settings show the effective legacy rule and the proposed new values before\nwriting both keys. Do not silently inject `always` (which differs for one-member\nmilestones), and do not silently disable review on an existing instance by parsing\nan absent field as `never`. A partially configured adopting policy is invalid.\n\nAfter adoption, `pose close` remains an explicit operation for every milestone\nand roadmap in all three modes, including scopes whose review is inherited or\ndispensed with. Persist a closeout receipt containing `review: not-required`,\nthe exact policy key/value, evaluator version and reason/counts when exempt.\nSatisfying member reviews or reaching the last member does not itself perform\nthis close. Member reviews and edge/exit-gate validation remain required.\n\nOptional reviews use the same verification as required reviews. An authentic\nnegative review retains its veto until remediated under the existing rules;\nchanging a setting cannot erase it. No exemption may weaken a stronger protected\nbaseline or mandatory-human requirement. `doctor` reports open scopes newly owing\nreview after policy or composition changes; the close gate enforces the result.\n\nVerify scopes already sealed as done against their sealed policy and plan,\nincluding legacy inherited milestones. Never backfill a fictitious exemption,\nreinterpret an existing receipt, or invalidate historical approval merely because\nthe current setting differs. Apply the new close and exemption rules prospectively.\n\n## Consequences\n\nNew instances skip aggregate reviews by default while still closing each scope\nand recording why review was not required. `when-justified` preserves review for\nthe compositions the maintainer selected. Explicit `always` adds cost even for\na one-member milestone; that is deliberate and visible.\n\nImplementation touches review-policy loading, common scope evaluation, persisted\ncloseout evidence, setup/settings, doctor, CLI/MCP projections and their tests.\nThe existing one-member ADR is superseded only for newly adopting open scopes;\nits sealed historical outcomes remain valid. No automatic instance adoption,\nhuman approval or delivery attestation is implied by this proposed ADR.\n\nRejected alternatives:\n\n- Boolean switches: cannot express the requested middle mode.\n- An agent estimates the benefit: yields irreproducible gates.\n- Require review with two milestone members: contradicts the chosen threshold.\n- Default existing missing fields directly to `never`: silently changes authority.\n- Treat `always` as legacy behavior: hides the one-member exception.\n- Ignore a recorded rejection after switching to `never`: launders negative evidence.\n- Recompute done scopes using current policy: breaks historical sealed approvals.\n- Remove close when review is exempt: conflates lifecycle and review requirements.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
