{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_29","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n 'closeout-review|CloseoutReview|receipt|never|always' pose-mcp/internal/cli/testdata/adversarial/README.md POSE.md locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\"","aggregated_output":"locales/pt-BR/POSE.md:228:# todo gate acima, mais `pose index` e `pose state`, também aceita: [--json] [--json-out f] [--quiet] [--color auto|always|never]\nlocales/pt-BR/POSE.md:378:  **Política de revisão do fechamento (schema 5):** uma instância que adota `pose adopt closeout-review-policy` decide com `milestone_review_required` e `roadmap_review_required` (`never`, `when-justified`, `always`) se o fechamento de um milestone ou de um roadmap exige revisão própria. `when-justified` exige para milestone com mais de duas specs distintas e para roadmap com mais de uma spec distinta e ao menos uma associação de ADR (`adrs: [adr:<arquivo>.md]` no roadmap, `roadmaps: [roadmap:<slug>]` na ADR, ou um link Markdown local em qualquer direção, lido como o CommonMark o lê). `always` exige inclusive para milestone de um membro, e nem a herança do membro único nem `unreviewed_milestones` a enfraquecem. A revisão de cada membro continua exigida; uma revisão registrada no escopo mesmo assim é verificada como antes e sua rejeição vale; membro ou associação que não resolve nunca concede dispensa. Um milestone que fecha sem revisão própria mantém o seu exit gate: o gate vai para o plano de revisão do seu único membro, ou do último (o selado quando todos os outros já são terminais), e o fechamento fica bloqueado até que a revisão aprovada de um membro tenha julgado o gate como ele está, ou até que o milestone seja revisado. Um membro movido por transferência conta no destino, e um milestone ou roadmap citado em `after:`, `consumes:` ou no `depends_on` de uma spec só satisfaz a aresta, e só deixa a spec dependente pronta, depois de fechado sob a política adotada do seu projeto, não pelo status dos membros; um roadmap done fechado pelo recibo volta a oferecer os seus milestones se o fechamento deixar de se verificar. Com a política adotada, `pose close milestone:<roadmap>/<id>` e `pose close roadmap:<slug>` continuam explícitos em todos os modos e publicam um recibo (`review-closeouts/<milestone|roadmap>/…json` dentro de `.pose/`) com `review: required|not-required`, a chave, o valor, `closeout-review@1` e o motivo (`members=1, threshold>2`); o escopo só é terminal com ele. Instalações novas adotam `never`/`never`; uma policy existente mantém a regra legada até adotar, `pose setup` mostra as duas, `pose doctor` lista escopos abertos que devem revisão, e roadmaps fechados antes da adoção são verificados pelo contrato com que foram selados.\nlocales/pt-BR/POSE.md:811:registros históricos e não cria receipts retrospectivos.\nlocales/pt-BR/POSE.md:1075:Leia a mesma assurance atual por verify, review-check, MCP e `pose state --attention`: `machine-attested` identifica a declaração assinada, sua fonte, run/proof e comparação de vendor/modelo. Pins atuais, projeto/audience/tempo, inputs selados, artefatos retidos e receipts originais/de continuação são revalidados. Revogação, receipt ausente, dados reescritos ou inputs stale retiram a assurance de máquina. Assinar não restaura quota nem apaga runs negativos anteriores. O worktree de origem fica registrado com o run, não é exigido de quem lê: uma prova verifica em qualquer checkout do projeto, então um run feito por um job de CI ou pelo Conductor é verificado como um local; não há alegação de contenção universal.\npose-mcp/internal/cli/testdata/adversarial/README.md:36:| `unpinned-premise-trigger-reads-current` | enforced | A premise trigger without a pin can never go stale and nothing says so | pose-assumption-validity-scope |\npose-mcp/internal/cli/testdata/adversarial/README.md:38:| `unobserved-falsifier-reads-uncontradicted` | enforced | An unbound legacy index cannot prove that a falsifier check was never observed | pose-falsifier-reconsideration |\npose-mcp/internal/cli/testdata/adversarial/README.md:47:| `run-pattern-names-no-test` | delegated | A validation check's `-run` alternative names a test that no longer exists, or never did, and the check passes on Go's exit 0 — through `-run=`, a build-excluded or ignored file, a vendored or nested-module declaration, a benchmark or a name Go does not run (`cli/validation_matrix_patterns_test.go:TestDeadRunPatternGuardFollowsGoSelection`, `cli/validation_matrix_patterns_test.go:TestValidationMatrixRunPatternsNameExistingTests`); a non-plain pattern passes only when listed with its reason | pose-validation-matrix-dead-test-patterns |\npose-mcp/internal/cli/testdata/adversarial/README.md:53:| `deleted-reservation-resets-quota` | delegated | Deleting a reservation and receipt makes a surviving run stop consuming quota | pose-delegated-review-attempt-ledger |\npose-mcp/internal/cli/testdata/adversarial/README.md:56:| `canned-executor-approval-proves-cognition` | delegated | A complete canned approval from a trusted local command is presented as proof of review cognition or a vendor receipt (`cli/delegated_review_capability_test.go:TestDelegatedReviewCapabilityCannedApprovalDisclosesItsLimits` guards disclosure; the engine cannot detect canned judgments) | pose-delegated-review-capability |\nPOSE.md:28:5. **Risk transparency**: always surface gaps and human-review points.\nPOSE.md:119:  `blocked` while a destination is staged. It is never a delivery outcome, and\nPOSE.md:124:  list** (POSE frontmatter is flat by contract — never a multi-line YAML\nPOSE.md:146:  ignored, so the item reads as `unowned` and never becomes overdue;\nPOSE.md:212:# basis nodes read-only; it is advisory and never changes lifecycle state.\nPOSE.md:224:# every gate above, plus `pose index` and `pose state`, also takes: [--json] [--json-out f] [--quiet] [--color auto|always|never]\nPOSE.md:323:                                   # apart, never a failure), warn, error\nPOSE.md:345:- `spec-format` — inspects and migrates specifications to the modern chronological date-prefixed format (`migrate <slug>|--all [--format folder|flat] [--dry-run]`, `status`). Enforces mandatory directory envelope preservation whenever companion artifacts (`amendments.jsonl`) exist.\nPOSE.md:346:- `spec-transfer` — explicit cross-project authority transfer. `preview` emits a deterministic digest-bound JSON plan without writing. Requirement mappings are explicit (`R1=equivalent:R1`, `R2=withdrawn`, `-=pending:R3`); apply requires the same digest plus one `--authorize-project` for every affected repository, and resume verifies the saved plan copies and receipts. Transfer is refused until both endpoints and every affected project adopt review-policy schema 4 (`qualified_artifact_refs_version: 1`, `spec_authority_transfer_version: 1`). Current dependency/membership links follow the verified redirect; the MCP tool `pose_spec_transfer_status` reads the journal only within its authorized project. `--mode reconcile-terminal` retires a coordinator spec onto an executor that is already `done` with a terminal closeout in its own project: the executor file is never written (apply only verifies its digest), the map is N:M over every source requirement and may target another spec with `destination_ref`, `withdrawn` needs a `rationale`, and `pending` needs a `rationale` plus an open target spec, recorded in the schema-2 redirect as `open_obligations`; `--map-file` reads the map as JSON. Engines without this mode refuse schema-2 plans and redirects. No automatic commits, pushes or policy adoption occur. Split-section specs without a single `spec.md` are reported as unsupported rather than rewritten.\nPOSE.md:351:- `validate` — runs the declarative matrix in [`validation-matrix.json`](.pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json): per-stack checks, per-module overrides, severity (`required`/`optional`) and mode (`strict`/`tolerant`). `--json-out`/`--junit`/`--sarif <path>` (`--json <path>` still works as a deprecated alias of `--json-out <path>`) emit the versioned structured result (schema 1) from one canonical model: stable check IDs (`<module>/<stack>/<name>`), command metadata, timing, severity, distinguishable outcomes (`pass|fail|error|skipped` — infra failures never masquerade as check failures), deterministic skip reasons, bounded captured output and secret redaction (configured env values only; inherited environment never enters the result). Text output stays authoritative; machine formats are additive. POSE-specific semantics survive the JUnit/SARIF projections via documented extensions (classname suffix / `pose/*` properties).\nPOSE.md:352:  **Runtime guardrails:** every check runs under a timeout (`timeoutSeconds` per check, `defaults.timeoutSeconds`, safe default 600s) and an output ceiling (`defaults.maxOutputBytes`, default 1 MiB); breaching either terminates the process group and records the explicit state (`limit_state: timeout|output-limit`). Checks marked `isolation: \"required\"` never run locally — they are skipped with a machine-readable reason and exported by `--emit-plan <file>`: an execution-plan envelope binding project, spec, check plan, matrix digest, git HEAD and an approval slot to be stamped with an expiring execution identity before the Harness may run it.\nPOSE.md:353:  **Changed scope:** `--changed-from <rev> [--changed-to <rev>]` selects the minimum safe module set deterministically — modules containing changed files (tracked and untracked), transitive dependents via `dependsOn` edges in [`module-metadata.json`](.pose/indexes/module-metadata.json), and policy widening (criticality `high` always runs). A change outside every module runs everything (uncertainty prefers safe execution); unselected checks are recorded as skipped with the selection reason and `--explain` prints every decision. Revisions are confined to a safe grammar; without the flags, full validation is unchanged.\nPOSE.md:359:- `update` — single-command complete update: automatically checks and updates the `pose` binary executable to the latest GitHub release, refreshes scaffolds, rules, workflows and MCP config (`--force`), and migrates the repository instance schema (`.pose/schema-version`). A plain update merges `POSE.md`/`AGENTS.md` (instance-owned sections keep their content; an instance edit a merge cannot keep is saved to `<file>.pose-backup`, and a section that still matches what POSE last wrote is simply replaced), delivers machinery — backing up only files the instance edited since POSE delivered them, as recorded by digest in `.pose/state/machinery-manifest.json` — and seeds absent instance config; `--force` additionally reruns `install` over the instance, which resets the manuals wholesale. Use `--dry-run` to preview or `--no-self` to skip the binary self-update. A run that delivered its files but finds the instance's own state invalid reports that through the final gate and does not roll back. Downgrade is always refused.\nPOSE.md:363:- `recurrence-check` — scans [`history JSONL`](.pose/reports/) for `task_slug`s with `≥ --threshold` occurrences in `--window-days` (default 3 in 14d). Ignores `outcome=pass` by default (problematic recurrence is repeated failure), but a `pass` settles the earlier failures of its own `stable_hash` cluster: only failures after a cluster's latest `pass`, or with no `pass` in the window, count, and a pass of another `stable_hash` settles nothing. Settled clusters are not flagged and are listed as `resolved` findings with `recurrence.resolved_clusters`; `--include-pass` counts every record as before. A task whose consecutive runs switch between passing and not passing at least `--flap-threshold` times in the window (default 4) is reported as a `flapping` warning with `recurrence.flapping_keys` (spec `pose-recurrence-flapping-signal`); it never changes the exit code, because an unstable outcome is a signal to investigate, not a recurrence. When flagged, points to [`recurrence-escalation.md`](.pose/workflows/recurrence-escalation.md).\nPOSE.md:364:- `recurrence-effect` — closes the feedback edge: `--register` ties an escalation to its intervention (`rule:|workflow:|spec:<name>`) and observation window in append-only `interventions.jsonl`; the report compares recurrence rate (and optional `pose report --duration-seconds/--cost-usd` telemetry) before/after per intervention with data-quality warnings (sparse sample, incomplete window). `INEFFECTIVE` verdicts demand a governed follow-up; `--fail-ineffective` makes that blocking by policy. Aggregation is by task/context only — never individuals.\nPOSE.md:365:- `extension install|list|remove|verify` — signed extension lifecycle (spec pose-extension-catalog-lifecycle): a package is a directory with `extension.json` (id, version, kind: `skill|workflow|rule|import-adapter`, `pose_schema_range`, `files`, `permissions`, optional `conflicts_with`, `provenance`) plus `files/<repo-relative-target>` for every declared path. Targets are confined to `.agents/skills/`, `.pose/workflows/`, `.pose/rules/`, `.pose/templates/` and must fall inside the package's own declared `permissions`. Extensions are **data only** — the lifecycle never executes anything from a package. `install`/`remove` are dry-runnable (`--dry-run`), require explicit consent (`--yes`) and are transactional: any mid-transaction failure rolls back every file already written. A conflicting target (owned by another extension, or an existing untracked file) blocks the operation unless `--force`; a locally modified managed file blocks `remove` unless `--force` (user modifications are preserved by default). Unsigned packages are rejected unless `--allow-unsigned` is passed explicitly; signature verification runs `cosign verify-blob` against the identity the package's own `provenance.signer`/`provenance.issuer` declare. Installed extensions are recorded in `.pose/indexes/extensions.lock.json` (id, version, per-file digest, provenance, signature status) — read-only over MCP via `pose_extension_list`; `install`/`remove` stay CLI-only by design (POSE never exposes general-purpose write tools over MCP). A `revoked: true` manifest is always rejected.\nPOSE.md:368:- `followups` — aggregates all specs' follow-ups, derives the live (`--open`) or full (`--all`) backlog, projects ownership (`--owner <alias>`) and expired reviews (`--overdue`), and proposes near-duplicate candidates by deterministic lexical similarity (token Jaccard + `SequenceMatcher`, stdlib only; threshold via `--similarity 0..100`, default 60). Exit 0 by default, no network; `--fail-overdue` turns expired reviews into a blocking, risk-based policy gate. The candidates are mechanical hints — the **semantic judgement** and the **confirmation of reuse** live in the agent layer (skill `pose-spec-closeout`), never in this script. Source coverage (spec `pose-source-coverage-integrity`): the read reports what it could not see. `--json` carries `coverage_complete` and `coverage` (each loss names the artifact, the kind, and the spec it belongs to when the file name or a readable header shows it), the human output prints `coverage=INCOMPLETE` and each unread file, and `--fail-overdue` fails instead of certifying \"none overdue\" over an incomplete read. The backlog, `--candidates`, the project state and `pose state --attention` count one unresolved set: an explicit `[open]` and a bullet with no disposition (Attention projects the latter as `followup-undisposed`). The same applies to `pose assess tech-debt`, whose report states the files scanned, the ones it could not read (incomplete) and the ones it leaves out on purpose (over the size limit, binary, generated), and finds a marker after a very long line; and to the review history: `pose review-check` and `pose review verify` do not take the latest readable attempt or attestation as the current one while an unreadable record that may be later exists, and `pose review record` refuses to choose the attempt it supersedes from a history with a hole. The obligation report marks a producer `incomplete` when it lost an element (an obligation refused by validation, a spec or review record it could not read).\nPOSE.md:369:- `review-plan` / `review-check` / `closeout-check` — resolve a deterministic component-aware plan from governed metadata, typed overlays and a closed native-tool catalog, then validate immutable attempts against both scope and plan digests. Opt in explicitly with review-policy schema v2, `component_aware: true` and `component_aware_adopted_at`; preview the migration read-only with `pose review-plan <scope> --explain` before committing the policy. Completed attempts older than that component adoption date remain auditable, while open scopes require the new plan. `pose review record` is dry-run by default, accepts `--plan-digest` to reject drift and appends with `--apply`; recommended tools are never executed implicitly. Hierarchical closeout rolls approvals through `spec:`, `milestone:` and `roadmap:` scopes. Read-only MCP: `pose_review_plan`, `pose_closeout_state`. The JSON of `closeout-check` and `pose_closeout_state` types its blockers in `diagnostics` (code, domain, refs, satisfaction condition; text blockers the engine cannot type yet are carried as `opaque`) and its next action in `next_step`; readiness `waiting_on` entries carry a `code`, and auto-attest pendencies a `code`, `source` and `condition` (spec `pose-typed-producer-diagnostics`). The prose fields stay for people; consumers read the codes.\nPOSE.md:370:- `review bundle` / `review attest` / `review auto-attest` / `review verify` / `review record` — opt-in fixed-point review (`review_bundles: true` plus an adoption date). Evaluation is a DAG, and each stage names the one before it: the implementation subject carries an `implementation_digest` over its classified content alone — not over change-set ids or refs, so a squash or rebase of the same content keeps its identity — and an observer of the implementation anchors there rather than on the bundle that will contain it. Sealed evidence records `subject_observation` as `observed`, `carried-forward` or `unknown`; a result naming no commit is unknown, never current. A change set's range observation reports how many commits `base..head` spans against how many it attributes, because paths resolved from trailers are attributed while the range between them is only provenance; it sits outside the sealed payload, so an unrelated commit never stales a review of content that did not move. A criterion is `mechanical` when it names evidence classes a registered check emits, and `judgment` otherwise: `auto-attest` answers the first kind from the sealed evidence and reports the second as pending, refusing `--apply` until a reviewer records a conclusion for each. A profile may declare `kind` explicitly to ask for judgment on a criterion a check also covers; it may not declare `mechanical` on one naming no evidence class. A component-scoped tool whose component the validation matrix declares runs no check (`replaceDefaultChecks` with an empty list) is planned with `producer_coverage: none` and may be disposed `not-used` with a reason, because nothing in the repository can emit what it asks for; everywhere else a required tool must still pass. Preparation hashes the semantic Intent, Requirements, Technical Plan and Decisions, attributed patch/tree content, consumed plan inputs, validation identities and child bundles. Tasks, execution logs, Final Report, lifecycle metadata, attestations and derived state remain outside the review digest but retain their own gates. `--seal` persists immutable JSON in the review-bundles directory under an `rvb-` identity; attestations are separate append-only JSON in review-attestations under an `rva-` identity. `auto-attest` extracts matching evidence from validation results and resolves tool dispositions deterministically. Semantic or source changes create a superseding bundle and typed delta; derived closeout changes do not cause rereview. Local review stays offline. Conductor may return an optional Ed25519 envelope only when its issuer/public-key pin is trusted by policy. Human plan output groups repeated path warnings and separates active required, recommended and completion-deferred tools; JSON retains full provenance. Sealing also records the governance contracts in force (`component-aware`, `review-bundles`, `evidence-vocabulary`) and the gates that decide the bundle (reservations, accepted-risk severities, criterion reuse); verification reads them from the bundle, and only `require_signed_attestations` is read live. Sealing warns about evidence produced against a commit other than the approved head. Sealing also records the implementation identity: principal, vendor, model, a digest of the sealing session's reference (never the reference) and whether it was `detected` from the session's agent markers or `declared` with `--seal --lead <vendor>[/<model>]`. The model comes from `--lead` or `routing.lead_models` and is `unknown` otherwise, which never establishes a model difference; markers of two vendors leave the vendor `unknown`. Resealing content a sealed bundle already holds returns that bundle with the identity it recorded, so a reviewer closing out never becomes the implementation; only a `--lead` naming another identity than the governing bundle's seals anew, and that seal names the bundle it replaces, so it governs whatever the seal instants say; a run compared with a replaced identity stops governing. The identity is sealed but is not content: a sealed bundle matches a preparation that differs from it only by the identity. An attestation's `passed` criterion must cite evidence the bundle contains, of a class the criterion accepts (any one of its `evidence_classes`), from the component it is about — evidence from a directory inside a component does not answer for the component. `--criterion ID|disposition|evidence|rationale` records `passed`, `not-applicable` (rationale required) or `finding` (names a recorded finding); `--finding ID|severity|disposition|action|evidence[|owner|rationale|review-by]` needs a severity and an action, and `accepted-risk` or `wont-fix` needs a sealed accepted severity, an owner, a rationale and a review date. Read-only MCP: `pose_review_bundle`. `pose_review_prepare` (read-only) returns the auto-attest draft and its pending judgment criteria; `pose_review_attest` (spec `pose-mcp-review-attest-signed-only`, `governance-write`) records an attestation over MCP only inside a trusted issuer's envelope, the proof `--envelope` verifies; its preview completes a draft for a sealed bundle (bundle digest, attested time, id and, when it names `confirmed_by`, the confirmation digest) and returns the exact bytes to sign. A human confirmation is disclosed as verified only when the signed authority claim names that human.\nPOSE.md:374:  **Closeout review policy (schema 5):** an instance that adopts `pose adopt closeout-review-policy` decides with `milestone_review_required` and `roadmap_review_required` (`never`, `when-justified`, `always`) whether the close of a milestone or a roadmap owes its own review. `when-justified` requires it for a milestone with more than two distinct specs, and for a roadmap with more than one distinct spec and at least one ADR association (`adrs: [adr:<file>.md]` on the roadmap, `roadmaps: [roadmap:<slug>]` on the ADR, or a local Markdown link either way, read as CommonMark reads it). `always` requires it even for a one-member milestone, and neither the singleton inheritance nor `unreviewed_milestones` weakens it. Member reviews stay required; a review recorded on the scope anyway is verified as before and its rejection stands; a member or association that cannot be resolved never grants an exemption. A milestone that closes without its own review keeps its exit gate: the gate is carried into the review plan of its only member, or of its last member (the one sealed once every other member is terminal), and the close is blocked until an approved member review judged the gate as it reads now, or the milestone is reviewed itself. A member a transfer moved counts at its destination, and a milestone or roadmap named by `after:`, `consumes:` or a spec's `depends_on` satisfies the edge, and makes a dependent spec ready, only once closed under its project's adopted policy, not by its members' status; a done roadmap closed by its receipt keeps its milestones reachable again if their closure stops verifying. Under the adopted policy `pose close milestone:<roadmap>/<id>` and `pose close roadmap:<slug>` stay explicit in every mode and publish a receipt (`review-closeouts/<milestone|roadmap>/…json` inside `.pose/`) with `review: required|not-required`, the key, the value, `closeout-review@1` and the reason (`members=1, threshold>2`); the scope is terminal only with it. New instances adopt `never`/`never`; an existing policy keeps the legacy rule until it adopts, `pose setup` shows both, `pose doctor` lists open scopes that owe a review, and roadmaps closed before adoption are verified by the contract they were sealed under.\nPOSE.md:377:- **Output channels** — the result (verdict, findings, data) goes to stdout and progress (steps, usage, the command's own failures) to stderr, so `pose x --json | jq` is safe and `2>/dev/null` never hides a finding. `--json` prints one document whose finding `severity` is the untranslated anchor; `--quiet` prints the verdict alone; `pose validate --verbose` streams a check's output instead of capturing it. Colour, symbols and the spinner exist only on a terminal, resolved per stream, and honour `--color`, `POSE_COLOR`, `NO_COLOR` and `TERM`; `COLUMNS` sets the wrap width and `POSE_LOCALE` (`en` or `pt-BR`) the language of every message POSE composes. Exit codes: 0 pass, 1 gate failure, 2 usage error.\nPOSE.md:378:- `artifact-check` — parses exact `### Artifacts` actions, resolves an explicit base/head range or `POSE-Spec: <slug>` commit trailers with safe structured Git arguments, and reports resolvability, existence, action mismatch, undeclared and orphan findings. A claim on a changelog fragment that `pose release prepare` archived resolves through the release manifest, which records the fragment's spec and digest; prepare never edits a spec. A file a later spec removes needs no longer exist for the earlier specs that declare it `created` or `modified`: their claim is satisfied — an edge `removed-by` in `delivery-integrity.json` — when the later spec declares `removed:` for the path, its attributed change sets contain a commit that deleted it (a rename is not a deletion), and every change the earlier spec made to the path — a merge, a root creation, a type change or a deletion included — is a Git ancestor of that commit; a change after the removal, a removal its spec never performed, a change set naming something other than an immutable commit, or history Git cannot read — a shallow clone included; replace refs and grafts are ignored — keeps the existence finding, and the finding says why. Commits modifying artifacts declared by a spec must carry a `POSE-Spec: <slug>` trailer in their commit message for `artifact-check` and `pose close` to attribute the changes. `pose report --change-from/--change-to` persists immutable change-set evidence; `pose index` projects claims, observations, reverse provenance and findings into `delivery-integrity.json`. The seven files `pose index` writes are one generation (spec `pose-index-generation-integrity`): their bytes are written under `generations/<id>/` in the indexes directory, the legacy names are published from the same bytes, and the descriptor `generation.json` there — SHA-256 of each file plus the digests of the inputs they were computed from — is published last and is the commit. A reader fixes the generation the descriptor names and checks each file against it: a set that does not verify (a file missing, replaced, from another generation, or a descriptor it cannot read) is `unavailable`, with the reason, never an empty or mixed result; a repository that never ran `pose index` since this contract keeps working and is reported `legacy-unversioned`, with no claim of coherence. A generated index edited by hand has no effect while the generation's copy exists (readers read the generation), and in a clone without the copy it makes the set unavailable; either way `pose index --verify` names the file. `pose index --verify` reads without writing and exits 1 unless the set is current, the legacy names are those of the generation and its inputs (specs, roadmaps, policy, module metadata, validation matrix) still hold. A failed publication leaves the previous generation in force and a retry converges on the same identity; publishers hold a generation lock in the indexes directory (it never waits: a second `pose index` is refused with `index-generation-busy` and writes nothing, and a lock whose holder is proven dead is recovered), the first publication of a repository is marked pending so one that did not commit reads as `unavailable` and not as a mixture of two runs, an operation that reads several indexes (the review plan) fixes one generation for all of them, and a commit whose durability could not be confirmed is reported as committed; retention keeps the current generation and the newest others up to three, never anything younger than ten minutes, and a retention failure is a warning, not a failed publication. Limits: Git history is read by the delivery graph but not pinned by the descriptor, and a clone without the git-ignored `generations/` directory verifies the legacy names against the descriptor. MCP: `pose_delivery_integrity`.\nPOSE.md:381:- `state` (spec `pose-project-state-artifact`) — native project-state artifact: answers \"what is the current state of this project?\" in one read instead of scanning specs/roadmaps/follow-ups/capabilities/knowledge/reports every session. `curated` sections (executive summary, current direction — human prose, preserved verbatim) and `derived` sections (specs & roadmaps, follow-ups, capabilities, decisions & knowledge, validation & evidence, architecture — counts and typed pointers, never copied content). `init` scaffolds; `refresh` recomputes derived sections while preserving curated ones, stamping `generated_at`/`baseline_commit`, and publishes only over the state it read, so a curated edit made while it renders is a `publish-conflict`, never overwritten; a refresh that published the state and could not append its history says so (`state.published=`, refresh log `result: partial`), and the next refresh appends its own entry, while `pose state` reports it as `history-not-appended` (spec `pose-writer-coordination-and-atomic-publication`); `--if-stale` only redoes the work when the artifact is already `stale` (cheap to run on every CI build). With no subcommand, validates schema, staleness (age/commits since the last refresh, configurable policy), **per-section hash** — a hand-edited derived section fails nominally (`[TAMPERED]`) — and `refresh_pending` (see below). `diff` compares the last two refreshes. MCP equivalent: `pose_project_state` (`section` param fetches just one). Additive: a project without the artifact generated yet stays valid everywhere. The Architecture section reports `unavailable` in this version — no local GraphForge export producer exists yet.\nPOSE.md:383:- `state --attention` (specs `pose-obligation-projection`, `pose-state-attention`) — live, read-only answer to \"what is still owed, by whom, restricting which phase?\". Obligations are projected from the subsystems that own them — readiness (unmet `depends_on`, Definition of Ready, unexplained `blocked`), closeout and review of in-progress specs (an unapproved review, each judgment criterion a sealed bundle still owes, text blockers carried as `legacy-opaque`), start reconciliation when atomic start is adopted, and open follow-ups as advisory residual debt — never stored a second time. Project-level sources are projected too (spec `pose-attention-projects-every-source`), originating on `xref:<project>/project:<project>`: an open docs review mark (owed by the doc's owner until `pose docs-review resolve`), a capability mechanism with a stale trigger, a release newer than the newest verified one that is not yet verified, and an accepted-risk or follow-up finding past its review date in the latest legacy review record. Free-form investigation notes carry no state and are not a source. The render shows coverage first: a producer that failed, or action requests until adopted, makes the answer `INCOMPLETE`, so an empty group never reads as nothing owed, and a fresh install, once committed, reads `complete`. Then what needs `--actor` (or any person or role), then what restricts each phase (`start`, `execution`, `review`, `closeout`, `release`), then residual debt. `--json` returns the report — stable `obl-` ids, qualified refs, reason codes, conditions, recipients, effects, satisfaction, knowledge, waiting, a snapshot of revision/dirty tree/policy/contract/index digests, per-producer coverage — plus the `attention` grouping. MCP: `pose_obligations` returns the same ids from the same function. Attention is not a gate.\nPOSE.md:384:- Governance read views (spec `pose-governance-read-view-generation`) — inspect `snapshot.read_view` in `state --attention --json` and `pose_obligations`: its own `schema_version: 1`, observed-input ID, engine/parser versions and root bindings describe one operation. The enclosing report stays at schema version 1. Review and closeout JSON expose the same contract in `read_view`. The ID binds consumed bytes, directory observations, errors and absences; revision, dirty state, size and mtime alone do not prove equivalence. `current` describes the final audit instant only. Coherence, producer coverage and freshness are separate: `unknown` or incomplete coverage must never be read as nothing owed or as write authorization. A historical report without this binding remains readable and proves no current authorization.\nPOSE.md:387:- `action <open|list|show|resolve|cancel|waive|invalidate>` (specs `pose-action-requests`, `pose-action-request-resolution`) — material requests to a person or an external system that no other source records: a decision, an approval, an input, an external operation or an acceptance, with its question, options and consequences, recommendation, recipient (principal, role or `unassigned`), qualified targets (`requirement:R4` inside the origin spec, or `xref:…`), per-phase effects (`--effect closeout:block` lets implementation continue) and an optional subject (`--subject requirement:R4` or `path:<file>`) whose change invalidates an answer recorded for the old content. Open one only when a different answer would materially change execution, scope, authority, risk acceptance, closeout or publication and no authorization already given covers it. Each request is an append-only journal under `.pose/actions/`, read whole: a line with a second value, a duplicate field (keys differing only in case included), an unknown event type, another schema, a sequence gap, an empty line or no terminator is never part of a view; `action show|list` and MCP locate it (line, byte offset, length, SHA-256) as an `interrupted-tail`, with a truncation proposal for a person to review, or as `corrupt`, to restore from version control — the engine repairs nothing (spec `pose-journal-checkpoint-and-lock-recovery`); `open` is a preview until `--apply`. Targets are qualified with the current project id; when it is derived from the directory name (no `.pose/project.json`, `POSE_DEFAULT_PROJECT_ID` or `POSE_PROJECT_ROOTS` binding), `open --apply` says so (on stderr with `--json`), because a checkout under another name would record another project, and the obligation snapshot lists the same limitation. A resolution names the `--request-digest` it answers and the `--expected-revision` it read, carries an `--idempotency-key` (a retry is a no-op, the same key with other content is refused, a second answer against the same revision is refused) and comes from an actor holding the recipient role in `.pose/policy/actions.json` (`{\"schema_version\":1,\"roles\":{\"maintainer\":[\"human:<name>\"]},\"identity_assurance\":\"declared|verified\"}`); under `verified` it needs `--claim <file>` with an Ed25519 claim from an issuer pinned in the review policy, bound to project, audience, request digest, principal and answer (a human principal also needs `human_authority_issuers`). Answered is not satisfied: a declined approval, a rejected acceptance or a failed external operation leaves the condition unmet. The requester may cancel its own question; a waiver needs the recipient authority and a reason. Open requests are projected as `actor-action` obligations in `state --attention` and `pose_obligations`; MCP opens requests with `pose_action_open` (risk class `governance-write`, a preview unless `apply` is true) and reads them with `pose_action_requests`; `pose_action_resolve` (spec `pose-mcp-action-resolve-signed-only`, `governance-write`) records an answer only with the principal's proof — a signature by a key registered to it, or a trusted issuer's claim — and refuses a declared answer under any assurance; its preview returns the statement to sign and the `ssh-keygen` command.\nPOSE.md:388:- Action destination scoping (spec `pose-action-target-and-node-scoping`) — origin is provenance, targets select relevance, and effects select restrictions. An empty effect scope still means the whole source artifact; a roadmap or milestone expands its current members. Explicit scopes replace that default, so a request from alpha can restrict beta without blocking alpha. A named whole artifact is a whole restriction, not partial merely because it is qualified. Pending requests remain visible when their origin becomes terminal. Unknown membership or unreadable material is reported as limited coverage and never expands into every spec. CLI accepts `--effect-json '{\"phase\":\"start\",\"mode\":\"block\",\"scope\":[\"xref:project/spec:beta\"]}'`; MCP `effects` accepts the same object alongside legacy `\"start:block\"` strings. Targets and subjects resolve by project/artifact/kind/ID, and a subject is re-read in its recorded artifact. Under adopted agency readiness, new approvals and acceptances require an explicit `--subject` / `subject`; legacy unbound requests retain their journal and report that answers have no content-change detection. Policy-off creation and transition defaults remain unchanged. This compatibility tightening requires release review; no policy adoption happens here.\nPOSE.md:389:- Signed answers (spec `pose-signed-action-answers`) — a principal proves an answer with an SSH key the project registered, with no external service. `pose identity add [<principal>] --key <file.pub> [--role <role>] --apply` records the key under `keys` in `.pose/policy/actions.json` (only `ssh-ed25519` and `sk-ssh-ed25519@openssh.com`; a key belongs to one principal; a private key file is refused); without a principal it suggests `human:<name>` from the git identity, which is a name, not a proof. `pose identity list [--json]` shows principals, roles and fingerprints; `pose identity remove <principal> [--fingerprint <fp>] --apply` stops future answers by a key without touching roles or answers already recorded. `pose action resolve … --sign <private key>` signs with the user's own `ssh-keygen -Y sign -n pose-action-answer` (POSE never reads the key); `--signature <file>` attaches a signature made elsewhere over the exact bytes `pose action statement <act-id> --actor <p> --answer <a> --idempotency-key <k>` prints, which bind project, request id and digest, actor, answer and idempotency key. POSE verifies the SSHSIG natively, records statement, signature and key on the answer, and `pose action show` verifies it again from the journal. A signed answer is `verified` under any assurance; under `identity_assurance: verified` an answer needs a registered key's signature or a trusted issuer's claim — two emitters of identity proof, Harne8 being one issuer. Presence follows the explicit channel contract below. `pose doctor` (`actions.keys`) warns when a human role holder under verified assurance can prove no answer, or signs only with keys that cannot prove presence (an error under `require_presence`).\nPOSE.md:391:- Human presence (spec `pose-proof-presence-channel-parity`) — preserve roles and keys when explicitly migrating `.pose/policy/actions.json` to `schema_version: 2`. Presence defaults to disabled; when `require_presence: true`, choose `presence_scope: \"universal\"` or `\"sshsig\"`. Universal requires a registered SK signature with an authenticated user-present assertion for every human answer, including issuer-backed answers. SSHSIG scope requires it when an SSH signature is supplied; issuer-only answers are explicitly outside that scope and establish no presence. An issuer claim may accompany an SK signature, but every supplied proof must verify. Identity grants, Portal authentication and user-verification alone are not presence. Agents do not acquire human-presence assurance. Enabled schema-1 policy requires migration before new human answers; unknown scopes are refused and older engines reject schema 2. This is a breaking change, requiring major-release classification, without automatic policy adoption. Previews disclose current policy as not-evaluated and never request a touch. Historical reads reverify recorded proof, disclose the key's assertion separately from identity, and leave historical presence requirements unrecorded; they never impose today's scope retrospectively. A verified assertion does not attest hardware provenance or who touched the key.\nPOSE.md:393:- Onboarding spec (spec `pose-onboarding-spec`) — a new instance starts with a governed first spec, `.pose/specs/<date>-pose-onboarding.md` (draft, in the install's locale): adopting POSE is the project's first piece of work, with requirements for the declared identity, a maintainer able to prove answers, the commit gate, the reviewed capability defaults and the committed instance, tasks that point to `pose setup`, a decision recording the defaults, and checks (`pose doctor`, `pose check --strict`). It passes lint, the strict check and the Definition of Ready as written. `pose setup` names `pose start spec:pose-onboarding` first and, once every other step is done, `pose close spec:pose-onboarding`. Only an install that creates the instance writes it; `pose update` and a reinstall never create or overwrite it.\nPOSE.md:395:- Phase readiness (spec `pose-phase-scoped-readiness`) — `pose_spec_readiness` with `phases: true`, and `state --attention --scope spec:<slug>`, report per phase (`start`, `execution`, `review`, `closeout`, `release`) whether it is `clear`, `restricted`, `partially-restricted` on named nodes or `unknown`. A phase is `clear` only when no obligation restricts it and every producer that could restrict it was read; a restriction on `R4` alone is reported as partial with the note that independence of the rest is not demonstrated. `ready` keeps its legacy meaning: partial eligibility never turns it true.\nPOSE.md:396:- Governed capabilities (spec `pose-governed-capabilities-default-on-new-instances`) — agency readiness, contract nodes, atomic start and causality closeout with the `structural-materiality@1` overlay. A new instance adopts all four: when `pose install` creates `.pose/policy/review.json` it sets their keys, dates atomic start, causality closeout and the overlay with the install day, and logs each adoption. An instance that already has a review policy adopts nothing at install, and `pose update` never adopts, even when it seeds a missing policy. `pose adopt <capability>` previews the keys one capability needs and `--apply` writes them, dated today or `--date YYYY-MM-DD`, so work created before the date is not re-judged; adopting an adopted capability keeps its date, `--off` removes the same keys, and a result the review-policy reader would refuse is never written. `state --governance` names the command for each capability not adopted. Every adoptable capability is in one catalog (spec `pose-capability-catalog`): the four above, criterion reuse, the Definition of Ready, the review overlays (`overlay:<profile>`), qualified references and spec authority transfer (which raise `schema_version` as their contract requires), signed attestations and verified identity. `pose adopt --list` shows each with its state — `on`, `off`, `declined`, `deferred` or `needs-setup` —, what it changes day to day, since which version and whether a new instance gets it (`--json` for the same data); a new instance adopts every capability the catalog marks as a default, the Definition of Ready and criterion reuse included. A capability whose requirement is off, or whose prerequisite (a trusted issuer, an authority audience) is missing, is refused with what is missing; `--off` is refused while another adopted capability depends on it. `pose adopt <capability> --decline --reason <text>` or `--defer --reason <text>` records in `.pose/policy/adoption-decisions.json` that the project looked and chose not to adopt it now, so it is not asked again; adopting later clears it, and the file is never shipped to another instance. A new instance's role map in `.pose/policy/actions.json` names nobody, and `pose doctor` lists naming them as a next step. A fresh install reports no warning in `pose doctor`: milestone and roadmap profiles count only once the project has a roadmap, and evidence-class coverage reads only the required checks of the shipped stacks some module uses (every shipped test and build check declares its class).\nPOSE.md:398:- Spec transfer and action requests (spec `pose-transfer-preserves-obligations`) — `spec-transfer preview` lists the source spec's action requests in `action_requests` with a disposition: `invalidate-in-source` for an open or answered request, `history-only` for one already closed. When the source is retired, each open or answered request receives an invalidation event keyed to the operation, so a resumed transfer records it once; a request opened after the preview makes the plan stale. Requests and answers are bound to their project and grant nothing in the destination, which re-opens a request if it still applies; a same slug and requirement id in two projects never share a request.\nPOSE.md:399:- `close spec:<slug> --plan|--apply|--resume` (spec `pose-recoverable-closeout-plan`) — the closeout as an ordered, recoverable plan computed from the repository: context (an in-progress spec, no uncommitted change outside the paths the plan writes), evidence and index (validate into `results_path`, then `pose index`, as a pair), seal, mechanical attestation (`--reviewer` names the reviewing execution; never invented), judgment, verify and the guarded transition. `--plan` previews (`--json` too); `--apply [--digest <plan digest>]` refuses a plan that no longer matches the repository, runs the mechanical steps and stops with exit 3 at a pending judgment, listing it with the sealed evidence refs (`closeout_plan.evidence`) and an attest command filled with them, one `--tool` per required tool and one `--criterion` per pending judgment (`closeout_plan.attest`, spec `pose-quickstart-real-lifecycle`); a requirement trace that is incomplete — a requirement with no entry, an orphan or malformed entry, or requirements with no `### Requirement trace` — blocks the plan at its `trace` step, before anything is sealed, and `pose close` refuses the transition for the same reason, so a closed spec never fails the lint that follows; `--resume` re-plans after an interruption, so done steps are not repeated and no bundle or attestation is duplicated. A checkpoint under `.pose/closeout-plans/` records progress and is removed on completion; a removal that fails is reported. It is progress, never a receipt (spec `pose-journal-checkpoint-and-lock-recovery`): `--plan` shows it as `valid`, `corrupt`, `unsupported` or `unavailable`; an apply over one that does not read stops before any step, for a person to inspect and remove it; and one that records an evidence, index, seal, attestation or transition step the repository does not reflect is a conflict, until `--apply --digest <current plan>` starts a new run. The plan never answers a judgment, publishes or edits policy, and promises a recoverable operation, not a transaction over Git and external processes. Operation results (spec `pose-cli-mcp-operation-results`, ADR 2026-10-16): `--apply|--resume --json` writes exactly one JSON document — `operation`, `outcome` (`completed`, `already-satisfied`, `waiting`, `blocked`, `conflict`, `failed`), the `step` it stopped at, `steps_run`, `pending`, `next_action`, the `effects` the domain confirmed, the steps left `unconfirmed` after a failure, `resumable`, a typed `error.cause` and the plan in `data` — and the human output reads the same result. A call that runs no step reports `already-satisfied`, not `closed`: it is not a fresh verification, and `pose closeout-check` is. Two different modes, or `--digest`, `--reviewer` or `--skip-evidence` with `--plan`, exit 2 before anything runs; `pose start` refuses two of `--apply`, `--status` and `--cancel`, `--digest` outside `--apply` and `--cancel --json`; an apply that fails after recording its baseline reports the recorded phase as a confirmed effect and the same `--apply --digest` as its next action, which finishes from the start record a transition already published; a spec that has not moved is judged again by the current plan and its governed restrictions, and a baseline the repository has moved past is reported as a conflict to cancel and preview again, never as resumable; a failed apply lists every state it confirms as an effect (the baseline and, once published, the spec moved to `in-progress`) and in `steps_run` only the steps that call ran, and a start record left unreadable (a baseline write cut short) is reported with the `baseline` step unconfirmed and a next action to inspect and remove it, never repaired automatically. `pose close <scope>` without a plan mode refuses `--json`. An MCP gate (`pose_check`, `pose_lint_spec`, `pose_skills_check`) stopped by its deadline or a cancellation returns `isError` with `error_code: execution_inconclusive`, its cause and partial output, never `passed=false`. The usage journal records a stop on a reviewer as `waiting`, not `failed`, and never averages an unmeasured interval as 0 ms. The plan modes are a spec's: `pose close <scope> --plan|--apply|--resume` on a milestone or a roadmap exits 2 and says so. Any other scope closes with `pose close <scope>` and no plan flag (the guarded transition: it verifies the gate, which on a large repository takes minutes, and names the next closeout, `pose close roadmap:<slug>` once every milestone of a roadmap is closed); `pose closeout-check <scope>` reads whether it is terminal and is what `terminal=true` means for a milestone or a roadmap.\nPOSE.md:401:- Assessment freshness (spec `pose-adaptive-assessment-freshness`) — a component assessment records its binding: the committed tree of the component, the engine version and the validation-matrix digest. `pose assess discover --if-stale [--component <dir>]` reuses an assessment whose binding is unchanged and refreshes it otherwise, printing `assess.freshness.<component>` with the reason (content changed, engine changed, matrix changed, uncommitted changes, or no binding on an older record). Age alone never stales an assessment. A missing or stale assessment of a component an in-progress spec declares appears as an advisory `evidence` obligation in `state --attention`; it gates nothing.\nPOSE.md:402:- `followups --candidates [--json]` (spec `pose-followup-reconciliation-candidates`) — lists open follow-ups with a reconciliation reason: `target-terminal` (a spec the follow-up names is done, superseded or abandoned), `evidence-present` (a test it names now exists), `overdue` (its review date passed), plus lexical near-duplicate clusters. Each candidate shows its evidence and its limits; nothing is dispositioned, similarity never becomes `duplicate` without judgment, and an open follow-up never leaves Attention without a disposition written in its spec. Attention shows the same reason on the residual-debt item.\nPOSE.md:404:- Progressive spec surface (spec `pose-progressive-spec-surface`) — `pose new-spec <slug> --surface minimal|standard|full` records `surface:` in the frontmatter. A `minimal` spec keeps Intent, Requirements, the Technical Plan with its Artifacts, Validation with the requirement trace and the Final Report with its follow-ups; it drops the planner-local Tasks section and the report subsections that `pose specs facts <slug> [--json]` derives from the delivery index (paths of the attributed commits, checks that produced scoped evidence). `lint-spec` stops requiring Tasks for a minimal spec and relaxes nothing else: the trace, follow-up and amendment gates apply as before. The facts never include intent, rationale or accepted risk. Evidence binding (spec `pose-spec-facts-evidence-binding`): `specs facts` separates `checks` (results whose scope provenance names the spec, with their basis and whether they ran against the head of the spec's change sets) from `candidates` (results the index holds that nothing ties to the spec: a declared module or an empty `components` is not provenance); candidates are printed as `CANDIDATE, not evidence of this spec`. `freshness` reports `fresh`, `stale` (an input changed since the index was published), `unknown` (never published as a set) or `unavailable`, and a limitation says to run `pose index` when it cannot be established; reading the facts never rebuilds the index and never concludes that a requirement is satisfied.\nPOSE.md:405:- **Automatic project-state refresh** (spec `pose-project-state-refresh-contract`) — an internal post-event hook registry fires a **partial** `pose state refresh` (only the sections an event affects) at the points POSE already intercepts: a successful `pose lint-spec <slug> --strict` on a `status: done` spec (`spec_closeout` → Specs & Roadmaps, Follow-ups, Validation & Evidence), `pose amend` (`spec_amend` → Specs & Roadmaps, Decisions & Knowledge), `pose reconcile-evidence record` (`evidence_reconciled` → Validation & Evidence) and `pose assess snapshot` (`assessment_snapshot` → Capabilities). When the event carries a commit and `POSE_GRAPHFORGE_MCP_URL` is configured, the consumer calls `components_hit` (spec `graphforge-components-hit-contract`) from the state's baseline to the event's commit and attaches the hit components to the Architecture section (**directed** refresh); without GraphForge configured, refresh stays full/undirected — zero build coupling. No daemon, no filesystem watcher: it is a synchronous call at the exact point each event already happens. **Best-effort by default**: a failed refresh never blocks the command that fired the event — it marks `refresh_pending: <event>` in the state frontmatter instead (cleared by the next successful refresh, regardless of trigger). Strict mode is opt-in via policy (`strict_refresh: true`) — there, a refresh failure fails the triggering command. Every run (triggered or manual) is logged to the artifact's own append-only log (metadata only: trigger, target, result `ok|failed|skipped`, duration, changed-section hashes — never content). Dedup key `hash(event+target+commit)`: the same event processed twice (retry/replay) does not repeat the refresh, result `skipped`; `manual`/`ci` refreshes are never deduped against each other (an explicit call always runs). `release_cut` is registered in the event→sections map but has no producer inside pose-mcp today — cutting a release belongs to Conductor (a separate service); the trigger is ready for when that integration exists.\nPOSE.md:406:- **Capability reassessment triggers** (spec `pose-capability-assessment-triggers`, same hook registry as the automatic project-state refresh above) — the `assessment-staleness` consumer, registered on `spec_closeout`, resolves which components a closeout reached (via `components_hit` when `POSE_GRAPHFORGE_MCP_URL` is configured; fallback: intersecting the event's touched files against each mechanism's manually declared `paths:` globs in the assessment) and marks every affected capability mechanism stale — never mutating a score, only recording `since`/`trigger`/`hits` and projecting a cobrável demand (origin `assessment-trigger`) into `pose followups --open`, with no duplicate storage. `pose assess snapshot` clears the marks on the mechanisms it reassesses and records the mark→snapshot link in the history. On-demand: `pose assess stale [--json]` lists pending marks; `pose assess request --mechanism <id> [--reason <text>]` creates one manually (the same path a UI-driven \"flag for reassessment\" action would call over MCP). MCP tool: `pose_capability_stale`. Without GraphForge and without `paths:` declared, the event logs `capability_mapping_unavailable` to the refresh log — a visible signal, nothing marked silently. Anti-noise threshold (`min_hits`, hit `level` `direct`/`any`, the demand's default owner/SLA) is configurable, sharing the same policy file already used for age/commit-based staleness (optional; absent means these same conservative defaults).\nPOSE.md:408:- **Docs review-pending triggers** (spec `pose-docs-assessment-followups`, third consumer of the same hook registry as the two entries above — reused unmodified) — a `docs-review` consumer, registered on `spec_closeout`, resolves which components/files a closeout reached (`components_hit` when configured, matched against `owns:` entries declared as `component:<id>`; otherwise the event's touched files matched against each doc's `owns:` paths/globs, where a directory like `\"site\"` covers every file under it) and marks every doc whose declared area was reached as review-pending — never editing the doc itself, never touching a score. Marks accumulate in an append-only log outside the doc's own file and project a synthetic, owned demand into `pose followups --open` (origin `docs:<doc-path>`), reusing the manifest entry's own `owner` field when declared. `pose docs-review resolve <doc> [--no-change --reason <text>] [--commit <sha>]` closes every mark pending on a doc at once, recording `updated` (default, captures the current commit) or `no_change_needed` (reason required); `pose docs-review request <doc>`/`--all-stale` cover the on-demand path — the latter bridges every currently-stale doc into an active demand in one call. `docs-check`'s own output and `pose_docs_state` additively list what's still pending. Without a component map and without `owns:` declared, the event logs a visible signal instead of marking anything silently — here the path fallback is the mechanism's full-strength path (`owns:` is expressed as paths by default), not a lesser one. Anti-noise threshold (`min_hits`, hit `level`, default owner/SLA) is configurable, sharing the same policy shape as the capability triggers in its own file (optional; absent means these same conservative defaults).\nPOSE.md:412:- `stats governance` — schema 2 read-only projection over report history, sealed review bundles, attestations and explicit remediation declarations. It keeps preparation, judgment, intervention, freshness, telemetry, provenance and coverage independent, never emits a quality score, and reports missing/invalid data explicitly. `--report-type` filters validation attempts; `--band` filters reviews and finding links. Replay identity is `(report_type, task_slug, sequence)`; identity-less rows remain visible in coverage. MCP `pose_governance_stats` accepts the same selectors and requires `project_id` when choosing a federated root. Explicit finding links contain only opaque IDs, spec slugs, category and source band, with at most 100 rows and truncation reported.\nPOSE.md:413:- `stats governance --waits --rework` (spec `pose-governance-wait-rework-observability`) — two more dimensions beside the outcomes, never folded into a score. With `--waits` or `--rework` only the requested dimensions are computed; `--outcomes` adds the outcomes, which read the whole history. `--waits` times action requests and keeps three measures apart: the age of each request, the interval attributed to waiting on an actor or an external system, and `known_blocking_seconds`, the union per spec of intervals in which a request restricted the whole spec's execution (simultaneous requests count once). None of them is inactivity; missing timestamps are counted as unknown. `--rework` classifies superseded sealed bundles by what changed (subject, intent or plan, review plan, evidence, or unknown) and extra attestations on one bundle by review disagreement or an earlier attestation invalid against today's policy, leaving other causes unknown. `scripts/bench-governance.sh [runs] [pose-binary]` times `state --attention`, `check --strict` and `index` on the same revision and reports time and the index size separately, with the corpus revision and engine version.\nPOSE.md:414:- Premise validity (spec `pose-assumption-validity-scope`) — a material `Assumption A<N>` may declare `Valid scope:` (the context it holds in) and `Stale trigger:` entries `<kind>:<value>@<pin>`, where kind is `doc`, `report`, `knowledge`, `adr` or `snapshot` and the pin is a prefix (8+ hex) of the sha256 of the content the premise was judged against. `lint-spec --design-check` reports an unpinned trigger with the pin to append, an unreadable one as unknown, and a calendar trigger (`date:`, `ttl:`, …) as not evaluated: validity is by context, not expiry, and trivial premises need neither field. When pinned content changes, the obligation projection (producer `premises`) emits a `premise-stale` judgment obligation for draft and in-progress specs, advisory on review and scoped to the assumption and the decisions based on it. The recorded evidence stays; it is described as judged against the pinned content and not valid for the current one, and nothing is invalidated automatically. Provider or remote contracts are not monitored by the core. Trigger state is projection only and never changes the design digest.\nPOSE.md:415:- Falsifier observation (spec `pose-falsifier-reconsideration`) — a material `Decision D<N>` may add `Expected effect:` and `Falsifier check: check:<module>/<check>`, naming the indexed validation check whose failure contradicts it. When the delivery index holds a `fail` result for that check, the obligation projection (producer `falsifiers`) emits a `falsifier-observed` judgment obligation, advisory on review and scoped to the decision, for any spec that is not superseded or abandoned (decisions stay in force after done). The engine infers no cause and judges no architecture: the failure is an observation, the decision and its rationale are never edited, and a person either records why it still holds or opens a superseding decision. A passing or absent result raises nothing. `lint-spec --design-check` warns on a malformed reference and on a falsifier check without a stated falsifier.\nPOSE.md:416:- `migrate v7 --dry-run [--json]` (spec `pose-v7-legacy-cleanup-plan`) — read-only inventory of what a future major would convert, keep or refuse: spec layouts, `blocked` specs, legacy and unknown review policy keys, sealed bundles, attestations by identity mode and attribution, and incomplete transfers on this project's side, each with its action and removal criterion. It writes nothing. Sealed bundles, attestations and signatures are never rewritten. `--apply` is refused with the current fact: no apply is available in this engine, the only mode is `--dry-run`, which writes nothing; no future apply is promised.\nPOSE.md:417:- `assess design --spec <slug>` — read-only bounded projection over the canonical sealed review subject. It observes dependency, component, delivery-metadata, governance-contract and public-contract deltas, keeps unknown/unsupported coverage explicit, and never computes a complexity or architecture score. The MCP equivalent is `pose_design_delta`.\nPOSE.md:418:- `usage` — aggregates automatic, project-scoped CLI and MCP usage events: calls, execution/semantic outcomes, latency, findings observed and stable finding lifecycle (`unique`, `new`, `resolved`, `reopened`). Agents never maintain counters. Events are best-effort, local-only and stored outside the tracked worktree; raw arguments, output, paths, project/user identity and finding IDs are never persisted. `--since-days 0` includes all history; filter with `--tool` or `--surface`; MCP equivalent: `pose_usage`. This is product-usage evidence, not a DORA delivery outcome or an individual productivity score.\nPOSE.md:420:- `stacks [--path dir] [--json]` — read-only, offline catalog inspection (spec pose-stack-catalog-expansion). Matches directory entries against the maintained profile catalog (Node.js, Go, Rust, Java, **Python** — poetry/pipenv/pip/setuptools/pep517 — and **.NET**), reporting per profile: manager, marker, `winner`/`shadowed` (multiple managers present resolve by declared priority), `confidence` (`medium` under conflict), and whether the prerequisite native tool is on `PATH` — via `exec.LookPath`, never by executing a project file. Detected markers feed `discoverValidationModules`/`pose init --wizard` the same way Node/Go/Rust/Java already do; the actual checks run through [`validation-matrix.json`](.pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json) `python`/`dotnet` stacks. Python manager priority is expressed with `when.fileNotExistsAny` (skip when any higher-priority lockfile/marker exists) alongside the existing `fileExists`/`fileNotExists` predicates.\nPOSE.md:423:- `knowledge-housekeeping` / `reports-housekeeping` / `events-housekeeping` — idempotent maintenance (list/archive/purge). Mutations require `--apply`. Reports housekeeping **never touches `history/`**: the JSONL is the source of truth for `recurrence-check` and `report`'s temporal comparisons. Defaults: stale = 120d, archive purge = 365d.\nPOSE.md:425:- `start` — atomic start of a draft spec, journaled in a per-spec start record. Without flags it is a read-only, digest-bound preview: readiness and dependencies, the declared review obligations (no future structural delta needed) and the R/A/D baseline. `--apply --digest <sha256>` records that baseline and moves the spec from `draft` to `in-progress` under a per-spec lock with compare-and-swap; it needs `atomic_start_version: 1` in the review policy, repeats idempotently, resumes an interrupted run and refuses another plan for a started spec. `--cancel` undoes a start that never moved the spec. The per-spec lock records its owner (host, digests of the machine and boot IDs, PID namespace, PID and process start); a lock another call holds is reported, never broken by age, as `owner-active`, `owner-unknown` or `owner-dead` (spec `pose-journal-checkpoint-and-lock-recovery`). Dead is proven only on the same Linux machine — another boot, or a PID that is not running or now belongs to another process — and `--recover-lock <owner digest>`, with the digest `--status` shows, removes that lock only if its bytes and its dead owner are unchanged, leaving the start record to resume; an unknown owner (another host, an older engine's empty lock, another platform) is checked and removed by a person. The same lock is the spec's lifecycle lock: close and transfer take it too, so no two of them interleave on one spec (spec `pose-writer-coordination-and-atomic-publication`). `--status` classifies each node as `recorded-before-managed-execution`, `introduced-during-execution` or `legacy-unbaselined`, recomputes obligations and, under the capability, asks for reconciliation when status or the recorded baseline was edited by hand. `atomic_start_adopted_at: YYYY-MM-DD` (spec `pose-atomic-start-adoption-cutoff`) limits the capability to specs created on or after that date: an older spec in progress without a start is reported as `legacy-unbaselined` with a note and blocks nothing; without the date every such spec needs reconciliation. A start records observable precedence, not when anything was decided; MCP exposes the read-only view as `pose_start_status`.\nPOSE.md:426:- `knowledge-usage` — projects `knowledge:<slug>` citations from specs per artifact (owner, expiry, citing specs). Usage signals inform the owner's review; TTL is never extended automatically. Dangling `knowledge:` refs fail `knowledge-check`.\nPOSE.md:427:- `knowledge-suggest <query>` / `semantic-suggest <query>` / `suggest-feedback` / `portfolio-projection` / `reconcile-evidence` — deterministic lexical ranking and advisory projections for knowledge, feedback clustering, multi-roadmap portfolio milestones, and delivery validation evidence reconciliation. Suggestions never gate or auto-apply without confirmation.\nPOSE.md:430:- `context` — resolves a selected logical project and optional local or `xref:`-qualified task through the shared artifact resolver. The JSON result includes canonical authority, project/artifact revisions, resolution state, supported contracts and a `context_revision`; it never includes filesystem roots. Use this token to detect a changed checkout or revision before a cross-project write.\nPOSE.md:438:- `release` / `release-notes` — governed release lifecycle (`plan`, `prepare`, `check`, `notes`, `record`, `status`, `open-next`, `backfill`) ensuring immutable release notes, manifest freezing, provider evidence importing, and reproducible release verification. A release that introduces a governance contract carries a **Compatibility** section at the top of its notes, generated from the contract registry. `release-notes` provides a compatibility alias for viewing frozen notes. The version a cut is compared with comes from `version_source` in `.pose/policy/release.json` (`{\"path\": \"VERSION\", \"kind\": \"text\"}`, or `\"kind\": \"json\"` with a `key`): a project file holding `X.Y.Z`, with an optional `v` and `-dev` suffix. Only the engine repository may omit it; every other project must declare one. Manifest and tag integrity (spec `pose-release-manifest-tag-integrity`): a manifest is read through a strict contract (supported `schema_version`, no unknown fields, clean version and paths) that recomposes `specs`, `categories`, `breaking` and `release_input_digest` from the fragments it freezes, so an adulterated projection is refused instead of normalised. `check` and `status` verify a tag from its own tree: the tag (lightweight or annotated) is peeled to its commit, and the manifest, notes, frozen fragments and version source are read as blobs of that commit, never from the working tree. A tag whose tree holds no manifest is refused whatever the working tree holds; a later commit, provider evidence or the next cycle does not make a correct cut stale; a version source that cannot be recomputed from the manifest is reported as a limit.\nPOSE.md:440:  **Query cursors:** paginated MCP list tools (`pose_list_specs`, `pose_list_roadmaps`, `pose_list_knowledge`, `pose_list_reports`, `pose_capability_history`) continue a query over the collection it was issued for. A cursor is signed, expires after 15 minutes and is bound to its tool, project, filters and the ordered collection with its index generation; a changed collection, another query, an expired token, a version 1 token or a server restart is refused with a `cursor-*` code and the caller restarts from the first page, never continued over a changed collection. A page is at most 500 items, discloses its `generation`, `collection` and `total`, and a missing `next_cursor` does not certify anything beyond that collection (spec `pose-generation-bound-query-cursors`).\nPOSE.md:464:- Use only authorized logical IDs returned by `pose_mcp_context`; the tool never\nPOSE.md:587:- Privacy guarantee: staged reports strictly isolate POSE engine mechanics and never leak proprietary code.\nPOSE.md:618:no obligations: uncertainty is shown, never charged, and never read as low risk.\nPOSE.md:646:unknown reading is uncertainty, which `structure.unknown` shows and never charges.\nPOSE.md:689:verifies its identity, but is never written as an approval; the MCP preview of\nPOSE.md:694:are judged as they are, never rewritten.\nPOSE.md:698:requirement that pays for it — and never checks whether the causal claim is true.\nPOSE.md:701:not inapplicable, it is unmapped. Bundles sealed before the contract existed never\nPOSE.md:711:(`--mapping <criterion>|<delta>|accepted-risk|<why>|@owner|YYYY-MM-DD`) and never\nPOSE.md:724:spec without a creation date is never exempted, and an undated overlay applies to\nPOSE.md:766:never from the environment. `project` must equal `authority_project`, the project\nPOSE.md:781:as declarations. `cognitive_independence` is always `not-observable`.\nPOSE.md:798:trust. These fields never establish observed historical authorization. Expiry\nPOSE.md:815:rewrites no historical record and creates no receipt retrospectively.\nPOSE.md:819:An answer proof authorizes its answer only. New waiver, cancellation and supersession events carry `lifecycle_version: 1`. Under verified action policy they require an operation-bound SSHSIG (`pose-action-lifecycle`) or a trusted issuer's schema-2 action claim. MCP always requires this proof. Both supplied emitters must verify; human presence follows the current explicit channel scope.\nPOSE.md:825:Preserve the preview's `requested_at` using `--requested-at` (CLI) or `requested_at` (MCP); it is validated as RFC3339 and keeps successor identity stable while signing. This timestamp is request metadata, not authenticated wall-clock provenance. Opening a replacement with `--supersedes` requires the predecessor's requester or recipient authority. With verified policy, pass `--authority <project-relative JSON>` containing actor, request_digest, expected_revision, idempotency_key, reason and signature or claim/envelope, plus the exact successor_id/successor_digest returned by the open preview. MCP `pose_action_open` accepts the same `authority` object, always with proof. Creation alone never grants authority to retire the predecessor. Authorization is checked before creation and again under the predecessor journal lock. An authorized replacement can be opened but not retire the predecessor if an intervening revision/policy change prevents the append; the error names the partial opening. Cross-journal recovery belongs to B10.\nPOSE.md:827:MCP `pose_action_resolve` defaults to `operation: answered`; `cancelled` and `waived` require a reason and return a lifecycle statement in preview. Arbitrary actor `invalidate` calls are refused. Subject-change invalidation is derived by the engine from the original and current material digests; transfer invalidation remains a governed engine path. These observations do not require a new human signature and never grant waiver authority. No policy, issuer or consumer pin is adopted automatically. The stricter authorization semantics require release compatibility review before publication.\nPOSE.md:833:`next_step` in request views, presentation, obligations, readiness restrictions and closeout diagnostics, and `next_steps` in Attention, distinguishes `answer`, `replace-request`, `repair-subject`, `remediate-or-waive`, `resume-supersession` and `repair`. These are instructions and requirements, never proof of authority or automatic effects. An invalidated request requires a materially updated replacement via `pose action open --supersedes`; missing material needs repair. Decline remains pending: remediate or obtain an authorized waiver with `pose action waive` / MCP `pose_action_resolve` (`operation: waived`). Repeating identical material with a new timestamp or lineage is refused. Material digests detect declared content changes; they do not judge whether rewritten text constitutes adequate semantic remediation. A satisfied answer is not asked again. Partial recovery offers the same prepared `open`, never an answer against an old digest.\nPOSE.md:841:The first non-baseline `pose amend` on an empty history copies the valid completed atomic start baseline before recording the requested change. It uses the recorded hashes/state/relations, not edited text; the new baseline event records the current declared author/time, not past approval. Schema 1 keeps R nodes only; schema 2 requires `contract_nodes_version: 1`. An invalid or incomplete start refuses the append. No start means legacy behavior; an existing journal is never silently repaired. Explicit `--baseline` continues to snapshot the current state.\nPOSE.md:847:Lifecycle `done` records the transition; terminal verification reads the applicable review contract and current opted-in amendment/action/federation gates. `pose closeout-check`, `pose close --plan` and MCP `pose_closeout_state(include_plan=true)` share that verifier. A done plan exposes `lifecycle_done`, `verification` with `verification_basis` and `limitations`, and `next_action`; historical pipeline steps are `not-run`, not inferred as executed. `verify` reports the current query's verdict. `pose_closeout_state(include_plan=true)` also returns a typed `handoff` when the plan has steps left and nothing blocks it: the `pose close --apply --digest <full plan digest>` invocation as structured arguments, the arguments the performer still owes (`required_inputs`, the `--reviewer` identity when a mechanical attestation is pending), the project the caller selected (by its logical id and the digest of its root, never a path), the digests it was computed from, the authority it needs and when it stops being valid; it executes nothing and is not an approval (no handoff is returned for a plan that is terminal, blocked or on an unknown read view). Every MCP tool has a declared route class (domain-direct, cli-wrapper, read-only-planning, admitted-write, external-handoff), and `pose_suggest` and `pose_get_followups` run the CLI command in process unless `POSE_EXECUTABLE` is set or the installed binary was replaced since the server started, in which case they are spawned. Existing review cutoffs and sealed historical contracts remain applicable; a legacy exemption is disclosed without inventing an execution receipt.\nPOSE.md:849:For a done scope, `pose close --apply|--resume` is readonly: verified state returns `already-satisfied` with empty `steps_run` and `effects`, while missing required proof or invalid history returns blocked with a supported repair action. Neither outcome rewrites checkpoints, starts, amendments or lifecycle. Plain `pose close` also verifies instead of repeating the transition. An in-progress plan checks the existing amendment gate before sealing. No policy, review or historical receipt is created automatically.\nPOSE.md:857:attestation signing only, never human authority. The decision is enabled last.\nPOSE.md:894:requires inspection before retrying. An enabled decision never proves a vendor\nPOSE.md:895:receipt, human identity or cognitive independence. Use the\nPOSE.md:901:`pose review` governs the subject (bundle, brief, attestation, verify); `pose delegate` governs executions. An **agent** is a CLI profile configured in `.pose/policy/delegation.json`; a **run** is one execution; an **attempt** is a run and its continuations; a **thread** is the rounds of one scope. `pose delegate review`, `adjudicate` and `smoke` run the sealed bundle's generated brief on a disposable worktree at the sealed commit with read access only, under the attempt ledger and containment below; any other `--access` is refused. The agent never records an attestation: the engine records the conclusion of a completed review run only as the conclusions of delegated reviews below describe. Without `--agent` (or with `auto`) routing chooses among the agents of the purpose: first by `routing.prefer` (default `opposite-vendor-of-implementation`, in the order the policy declares its agents), then by the steps of `routing.on_unavailable` (default `same-vendor-other-model`, `wait`, `same-model`). `wait` applies only when every earlier candidate is limited with a known reset within `routing.max_wait` (default 5h) and starts the run after the first reset, without polling. The implementation routed against is the identity the bundle sealed; for a bundle sealed before bundles recorded it, the policy's `provenance.implementation`, else `--lead`. An agent named with `--agent` bypasses routing and warns when it shares the implementation's vendor. Every run records the decision (`routing`: the candidates, why each was skipped, the step taken) and `independence_achieved` (`different-vendor`, `different-model` or `same-model`); with no agent available the run does not start and the error carries the decision. `--label` names the run, `--lead <vendor>[/<model>]` declares the implementation when the bundle records none, `--dry-run` prints the resolved agent, purpose, access, working directory and argv (authorized credential values redacted) and reserves nothing, and `--json` prints one versioned document (`pose.delegate.run/v1`, `plan/v1`, `ls/v1`, `cancel/v1`, `agents/v1`, `error/v1`), usage errors included. On `ls`, `resume`, `cancel` and `agents` the same flags select: a run or an agent that does not match is not listed, or is refused when named; `--dry-run` changes nothing anywhere. Messages follow `POSE_LOCALE` (`en` or `pt-BR`). A run reference is a run id, an attempt id, a label or a session prefix of six characters or more; an ambiguous reference exits 2 and lists the candidates.\nPOSE.md:903:A schema-2 agent may opt in to `review_profile.command` and `review_profile.resume_command` for governed review only. Omission preserves its existing read adapter; tasks, adjudication and smoke keep their commands. A resumable agent must declare the profile's resumed command with `{session}`. Once selected, failure never falls back to the read or task profile. The selected commands remain bound by policy/provenance verification. Older strict parsers reject the new field. The configured Claude event codec canonicalizes repeated typed init records only for the same parsed session identity before session recognition. Different identities and generic regex ambiguity are still refused; raw provenance/redaction and limit/reset channels retain the original bytes.\nPOSE.md:907:`pose delegate ls` lists attempts by their latest run with state, purpose, agent, label, turns, age and last step — for an interrupted run also its cause, whether a partial draft exists, the reset instant and the time left — and the runs in progress with the liveness of their process. `pose delegate cancel <run>` stops a run in progress; it is recorded `failed` with the cause `cancelled`, and its attempt stays consumed. `pose delegate agents` lists each agent with vendor, model, whether its CLI is on the path, the reset instant of its last recorded usage limit and the state routing reads: `available`, `limited until <instant>` (its last run hit a usage limit whose reset is ahead), `missing` (its CLI is not on the path), `unknown` (the run history could not be read whole) or `probe-failed` (its optional `probe` failed within its bound). Availability is read from the path and the recorded runs; only a configured probe runs anything. `pose delegate review|adjudicate|smoke --detach` starts the run as a process of its own, in a session of its own that survives the caller's terminal, and returns at once with the attempt (a run reference) and the path of its log; a run whose route must first wait for a usage-limit reset is not detached. `pose delegate wait <run> [--timeout <d>] [--json]` is a plain foreground process that ends when the run does and prints one line — run, state, decision, resumable flag, reset instant — with the exit code `0` approved, `10` changes requested or rejected, `20` failed (a completed run with no decision too), `30` interrupted, `40` stalled, `50` the wait timed out, and `2` for a usage error; every waiter is released, a run already ended returns at once, and killing a wait leaves the run running. The process running an attempt rewrites a heartbeat in its marker every 10 seconds; a run whose heartbeat is older than three intervals and whose process is provably gone is recorded `stalled` by the first reader, through an exclusive claim every reader then agrees on, and `ls` shows each running run's heartbeat age. `pose delegate watch [<run>|<label>]` shows an attempt as one task: the runs it recorded are replayed with a marker where a run resumed another and the interrupted run naming the run that continued it, then a running attempt is followed through the normalized, redacted events its process writes as they come, until the record is published; `--no-follow` prints what exists and exits, `-v` adds the tail of each tool's output (`--lines <n>`, default 12) and `--json` prints NDJSON. With no reference it follows the only running run; at a terminal it otherwise opens fzf with a preview of the run under the cursor refreshed every `--refresh <d>` (default 2s, 0 turns it off), or a numbered menu without fzf; outside a terminal an absent reference exits 2 and lists the candidates. Colours appear only on a terminal and follow `NO_COLOR`. A live event is redacted with the credentials and the session references the stream has declared so far; the events bound to the record are derived again from the whole transcript. `on_terminal` on an agent (or else on a purpose) is a command run once after a run ends, with only `POSE_DELEGATE_RUN` and `POSE_DELEGATE_STATE` in its environment, the run directory as working directory, its own process group and a bound (`timeout`, default 30s, at most 5m); its failure is recorded beside the run, shown by `ls`, and never changes the run's state. `pose delegate handoff <run> --to <agent|auto>` continues a run that stopped interrupted or failed, short of a conclusion, with another agent under the same attempt and its continuation budget, which is the `resume.max_resumes` and `max_wall_time` of the agent that opened the attempt (3 and 60 minutes by default) whichever agent continues it, so changing agents never widens it; a completed run, the same agent and a run already continued are refused, and a run handed off is no longer resumed. Its brief is the predecessor's generated brief followed by a labelled section: the predecessor's partial final message and the commands it ran with their exit status, paired by the call identifier the agent's stream records and otherwise left unknown rather than guessed, each with its digest, quoted so no line of it reads as a decision, bounded at 16 KiB with the cut disclosed, and an instruction to re-verify. Only the handoff run concludes. The record binds the base brief digest and the section digest in `handoff_digest`, names its predecessor in `hands_off_from`, and a signed proof verifies the section against the predecessor's record. `--to auto` routes as the purpose does, the stopped agent excluded and unavailable ones skipped. A foreground run or handoff that stops interrupted in a terminal offers waiting for its reset and resuming, handing off, or cancelling, which is offered even alone, and does nothing until answered; the stopping command's result, `ls --json` and `wait --json` carry the same ways forward as `next_actions`, each with its command, whether it is available now — judged by the planning its command runs, budget and wait bound included — and why not, and a `cancel` that leaves the run stopped. `watch` marks a handoff with `⇄` beside the `↻` of a resume. `pose delegate task <prompt-file|->` hands any task to another agent: the prompt goes on stdin in `--cd` (default: the current directory) with the agent's command for `--access read|sandboxed|write|full`; the default is `purposes.task.default_access` (read when unset), `full` is never a default and prints a warning naming what it disables. A task's template places `{add_dirs}`, `{no_network}` and `{outside_git}`, filled from the agent's `add_dir_args` (with `{dir}`), `no_network_args` and `outside_git_args`; `--add-dir`, `--no-network` and `--effort` that the agent's template cannot carry are refused with exit 2, and `--no-network` with `write` or `full` too. The final message goes to stdout and the agent's exit code is returned; `--json` replaces both with one document. A task runs in the real directory: containment bounds its time and output, not its filesystem. Its record (prompt, stream, error stream, normalized events, argv, removed variable names, exit code, label) is owner-only under the user's state directory (`$XDG_STATE_HOME/pose/delegate/`, where `XDG_STATE_HOME` counts only as an absolute path and `~/.local/state` is the default), or, with `--record`, in the `delegate-runs` directory of `.pose`. The state directory may be reached through a link of the user's own; resolved, it and every directory above it must belong to the user or to root and be writable by no group or other user unless sticky, and `pose`, `pose/delegate` and the project's directory below it must be real directories of the user's that no group or other user can write; a task refuses to record or read anywhere else and names the directory (on Windows only links are refused). `--detach` starts a task as the review purposes start a detached run and returns its attempt, its log and the place of its record; a prompt piped to `task -` or `resume --prompt -` is read before detaching and written to the detached run through a pipe, never a file, and a refusal before the attempt is reserved returns the foreground's document and exit code. A detached call keeps its log and hand-back in `.pose/review-private/supervisors`, which, with every directory up to the project root, must be the user's and writable by no group or other user, and whose root's ancestors must belong to the user or root and be writable by no one else unless sticky; the root is resolved once and only that resolved root is used, a caller whose current directory is gone is refused, and a project's private task history is keyed by its resolved root (records an earlier engine kept under a linked spelling are still read). A detached call believes a hand-back only once a piped prompt is through and a run reserved the attempt it names. A checkout that fails this can still run in the foreground; on Windows only links and non-directories are refused, and ACLs are not checked. A task is never evidence: no review reader reads it, `pose review attest --run` refuses it by name, and `wait` returns 0 when the agent succeeded, with no decision. `ls`, `watch`, `wait`, `cancel`, `resume` and `handoff` work on tasks; `resume` without `--prompt` sends a default continuation message, with the agent's `resume.commands` for the task's access, and `--agent auto` routes against the launching session. A task can also continue a session POSE never recorded, such as one a personal launcher opened, with `--session <id> --agent <name>`: the agent's resume command for the access runs with that session, the prompt optional, and the record says it continued a session without keeping it. `pose review dispatch`, `resume` and `status`, the aliases this surface deprecated, were removed: each exits 2 and names its `pose delegate` replacement.\nPOSE.md:909:`delegation.json` is schema 2: `agents` (vendor, model, `cli`, `events`, per-access `commands`, `effort`, `timeout`, `limit`, `session`, `resume`, `executor`, `containment`, `on_terminal`, `probe` — `command` and an optional `timeout` up to 60s, default 10s, with no placeholder), `routing` (`prefer`, `on_unavailable`, `lead_models` as `{vendor: model}` in lower case, `max_wait` up to 24h), per-purpose budgets (`purposes.<review|adjudicate|smoke|task>.max_runs_per_bundle`, `timeout`) and `env_unset`. Command templates are argv arrays; `{output}`, `{session}` and `{effort}` are whole elements, never interpolated, and `{effort}` becomes the argv fragment of the agent's effort level. An agent's own `max_runs_per_bundle` wins over the purpose's; a purpose's `timeout` wins over the agent's. `pose update` converts `reviewers.json` (schema 1) into `delegation.json` deterministically and removes it: each adapter becomes an agent whose `read` command is the adapter's command, a budget all adapters share moves to the review-path purposes, and every agent projects exactly the adapter it came from. The converted file is archived outside `.pose/policy` and named in `migrated_from`, so a run recorded before the conversion keeps its policy baseline while `delegation.json` is exactly that conversion; editing `delegation.json` ends that, as any policy change does. The engine reads `reviewers.json` while `delegation.json` is absent and refuses when both exist. A schema 1 adapter that interpolates `{output}` or `{session}` inside an argument (in `sh -c` code, for example) is not converted: `pose update` warns and keeps `reviewers.json` until the template passes the placeholder as a whole element.\nPOSE.md:929:The child process receives none of the parent agent's session variables (`CLAUDECODE`, `CLAUDE_CODE_*` session markers, `CODEX_THREAD_ID`, `CODEX_SESSION_ID` and the other names Claude Code and Codex set for their children), nor any name in `env_unset`, even when the containment allowlist names them; the run records the removed names in `env_removed`, never their values. An agent's `events` (`codex-jsonl`, `claude-stream-json` or `plain`) selects the parser that turns its redacted stream into normalized events — turn, message, tool call, tool output, usage limit, final message, error — kept beside the run's transcript as `<run>.events.ndjson` and bound to the record by `events_digest`. An agent that writes no `{output}` file has its draft taken from the final message its stream carries. A run in progress is known by a private marker under `.pose/review-private/active/`, which names its process the way a start lock does.\nPOSE.md:933:A personal launcher — a script, skill or note of one person that runs another vendor's agent and follows its logs — retires once the parity check passes: `go test ./internal/cli -run 'LauncherParity|ActiveDocument' -count=1` in `pose-mcp`, registered as the `launcher-parity` check of the validation matrix. The distribution's parity matrix (`delegation-launchers.json`, in the POSE repository, not in an installed instance) maps every capability the delegation ADR lists for the launchers to the `pose delegate` command that replaces it and the tests that cover it; the check fails while a row has no command or test, names a test or a flag that does not exist, or the ADR lists a capability no row carries, and a second check fails while an active document, skill or help text names a replaced command or launcher outside a removal note. POSE never changes files outside the repository, so the operator retires the launcher:\nPOSE.md:936:2. Replace each use: a review with `pose delegate review`, an adjudication with `pose delegate adjudicate`, any other task with `pose delegate task`, a continuation of a run POSE recorded with `pose delegate resume` or `pose delegate handoff` (they reuse its agent, access and settings), a session the launcher left interrupted with `pose delegate task --session <id> --agent <name>` — passing the settings the launcher used (`--access`, `--cd`, `--add-dir`, `--effort`, `--no-network`), which POSE cannot recover from a session it never recorded; the run it records then resumes with `pose delegate resume`, reusing them — a background run with `--detach` and `pose delegate wait`, following with `pose delegate watch` and listing with `pose delegate ls`.\nPOSE.md:946:Reservations live in `.pose/review-attempts/`, final receipts link the run, and `.pose/review-run-ledger/` witnesses exact run bytes and transcript digests. Every publication uses the exclusive synced publisher. New runs carry `ledger_version: 1`, `attempt_id` and the optional reason; legacy schema-1 runs retain their original identities, with no invented reservation or signature. An incomplete reservation, missing witness/run/transcript, inconsistent receipt, unknown record or exhausted read budget is explicitly incomplete. Inspect it; no automatic deletion, truncation, repair or retry occurs. Upgrade writers sharing these directories together. Run continuity and authenticated provenance remain separate deliveries.\nPOSE.md:948:`pose history-check --strict` detects missing or rewritten witnessed runs and Git modifications/deletions of tracked immutable run, ledger and attempt files, including staged or committed changes. History containing only delegated records is supported. Git/filesystem trust is required: erasing unanchored evidence and its witnesses cannot be proved from that same erased storage. No cryptographic append-only storage or vendor identity is claimed. Records are limited to 1 MiB, inventories to 10000 entries, run JSON reads to 32 MiB per operation, transcripts to 256 MiB each and 512 MiB per verification, and Git history observation to 8 MiB/30 seconds; exceeding a bound is unknown, never a clean truncated history.\nPOSE.md:950:`pose review verify` and MCP disclose scope-wide prior runs alongside the attestation, including earlier rejecting drafts. `pose insights` aliases `pose stats`; text, JSON, HTML and MCP `pose_insights` show runs per bundle, attempts, reruns and per-adapter declared draft approval rates. A time window limits counted events, while the full preceding history classifies reruns. Approval rate uses unambiguous completed, unsuperseded `decision: approved|changes-requested|rejected` drafts; ambiguous output, failures and stale runs are unknown. Legacy unreserved runs count as individual attempts without claiming a reservation existed. Draft labels and rates are not verified approvals and never apply an attestation or enable delegated-review automatically.\nPOSE.md:952:**Optional worktree runtime.** `pose runtime start` starts, detached, a runtime for this worktree; `attach` and `status` handshake with it, `stop` drains it (`--drain <d>`, default 5s) and says how many requests in flight finished and how many were abandoned, `restart` is stop then start, and `serve` runs it in the foreground (`--idle <d>`, default 30m, stops an idle runtime; 0 never does). It is opt-in: no other command starts it, and every command runs standalone whether it runs or not. The runtime is identified by the user, the canonical worktree (symlinks resolved) and the Git common directory, so two worktrees of one repository, or two users, never share one; the handshake compares the engine, build, protocol and schema as well and an attach from another engine is refused naming the field. Its endpoint is a Unix domain socket (Linux, macOS) of mode 0600 in a 0700 directory owned by the user (`$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/pose`, else `pose-runtime-<uid>` under the temporary directory, or `POSE_RUNTIME_DIR`), whose peer the operating system identifies before a byte is read; a link, a loose mode, another owner or a file that is not a socket is refused, and a socket nobody answers on is replaced. There is no TCP and no token. Slice 1 hosts no domain operation: it answers handshake, status and stop, confers no authority, signature or presence, and every restart has a new epoch, inheriting nothing. On Windows, which has no named-pipe implementation here, `status` reports `unsupported` and every command runs standalone. `status` exits 0 for running, standalone and unsupported and 1 for an incompatible runtime; `attach` also exits 1 when there is nothing to attach to. `--json` prints the document `pose.runtime.status/1`.\nPOSE.md:954:**Hosted checks and the resident Git memo.** `pose runtime run [--envelope] [--timeout <d>] <command> [args...]` runs `review-check`, `closeout-check`, `artifact-check`, `surface-check` or `roadmap-check` in the runtime of the worktree when one is running, compatible and willing, and standalone in this process otherwise; the exit code and the output are the command's, and `--envelope` prints one document (`pose.runtime.run/1`) with them and with `via` (`runtime` or `standalone`), the `fallback_reason`, the typed `outcome` (`completed`; `error` when the command ran and ended but its output (over 2 MiB) is not sent back, with the command's exit code; or `unknown` for a deadline or a lost answer), the epoch and the memo hits. The runtime refuses, before the command starts, one it does not host and a caller whose environment digest (the `POSE_`, `HARNE8_`, `GIT_`, `CLAUDE` and `CODEX` variables, `XDG_STATE_HOME`, `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` and `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR`, the locale, `HOME`, `NO_COLOR` and the user) differs from its own, so the caller runs standalone; a command that reached the runtime and whose answer did not come back whole, or whose `--timeout` passed, may have run and is never repeated standalone unasked: it exits 1 and says so. The runtime runs hosted commands as the user that started it, in its worktree, with the output of a non-terminal, in the admission lanes described below; a command still waiting for its slot when its `--timeout` passes is refused, not started, so the caller runs it standalone. A runtime also keeps, between commands, the output of the Git reads that are pure functions of object ids or of the commit HEAD named when they ran, keyed by that commit and the shallow state (256 MiB, least recently used first; not for a linked worktree): the second `review-check` of this repository took about 10 s against about 25 s standalone, with the same output. The runtime admits hosted commands in two lanes, each with its own slots and a bounded queue: `short` (`surface-check`, about 10 ms on this repository) and `heavy` (the other hosted commands; `roadmap-check <slug>` takes minutes here), 2 and 1 slots by default and queues of 16 and 4; `pose runtime start|serve` take `--short-slots`, `--heavy-slots`, `--short-queue`, `--heavy-queue` and `--memo-mib` (default 256). A short query is not queued behind a heavy check, and a command that finds every slot of its lane busy and the queue full is refused before it waits and runs standalone. `status` and the handshake carry, per lane, `<lane>_slots`, `_queue_limit`, `_queued`, `_running`, `_admitted`, `_refused`, `_wait_ms_total` and `_wait_ms_max` in `metrics`. A measurement of the runtime on a real repository, with its thresholds fixed before the numbers, is `POSE_RUNTIME_MEASURE=<file> go test ./internal/cli -run TestMeasureTheRuntimeOnTheRealRepository`; its report is under `.pose/reports/`.\\n\\n**Review rounds.** The rounds of a scope are its sealed bundles that a completed delegated review concluded on, in the order those runs ended; `pose review verify`, `pose delegate ls` and `pose insights` show the round. From round 2, the generated brief (template `review-brief/2`; runs briefed with `review-brief/1` still verify) carries a `Previous round` section built from the ledger: every open finding with the identifier it keeps across rounds (`R<round>-<id>`), its severity, disposition and location, the previous run and its draft digest. The reviewer's draft is the fenced code block whose info string is `pose-draft`: JSON with `schema` `pose.review.draft/v1`, `decision` and `findings` (`id`, `severity` high|medium|low, `disposition` blocker|follow-up, `file`, `line`, `summary`), and from round 2 `prior_findings` (`id`, `status` fixed|partial|not-fixed|superseded, `evidence`) and `sweep`. A finding reported `partial` or `not-fixed` keeps its identifier in the next brief. A round-2-or-later draft that leaves an open finding unaccounted, calls one `fixed` without evidence or states no sweep is recorded with `draft_problems`, `wait` reports it as `invalid`, and it is never accepted as an approval. With `routing.rounds` `continue-thread` (the default) a round resumes the session the previous round ended with, through the agent's resume command; it opens a new session, recording why, when the agent or its vendor changes, the chain of drafts is broken, that session cannot be resumed, or the round reaches `routing.round_threshold` (5 by default), where `verify` also warns that the rounds are not converging. `cold-session` opens a new session every round. Each run records its `round` and `thread`; `pose insights` reports, per scope, findings per round, rounds to approval, and how prior findings were accounted for in continued and in cold rounds. A previous round whose record is not intact makes the brief refuse.\nPOSE.md:956:**Delegated runs over MCP.** `pose_delegate_start` (review, adjudicate or smoke), `pose_delegate_status`, `pose_delegate_wait`, `pose_delegate_resume` and `pose_delegate_handoff` run `pose delegate … --json` in the project the server resolved for the call, so every plan, refusal and run is the CLI's: a start, resume or handoff is always detached and returns once the run has reserved its attempt (without `apply` it previews), a status never blocks, and a wait blocks at most its `timeout_seconds` (300 at most), returning `still-running` when the bound passes. Refusals carry the same `reason_code` as the CLI's error document (`rerun-without-reason`, `budget-exhausted`, `containment-incompatible`, `agent-unavailable`, `no-agent`), and results carry the normalized summary only: no transcript, prompt, session reference or environment value. `pose delegate resume` and `handoff` take `--detach` as the purposes do. A task is not started over MCP.\nPOSE.md:1017:Keep environment/credentials lists unique ignoring case (at most 128/32 names). Ambient token variables are excluded. Explicit profiles use a private disposable HOME; an omitted profile is disclosed as `legacy-trusted` and retains HOME compatibility. This is environment filtering, not protection against an executable reading same-user files. Authorize credential variable names explicitly; never put their values in policy, argv, brief or notes. Loader hooks and Git redirection variables cannot be allowlisted. Credential values and captured session references are redacted together in one bounded pass, longest match first; this is exact-value redaction, not general data-loss prevention or a guarantee against encoded secrets emitted by an untrusted adapter.\nPOSE.md:1023:Inspect `safety` on the immutable run, CLI dispatch/verify/resume and the existing MCP review-bundle projection. It reports profile/platform, available/unavailable capabilities, limits, capture completeness, execution cause, runtime resource identifier and the B12 cleanup OperationResult. Cleanup verifies operation ownership and removes only the captured workspace and its exact Git registration through confined descriptors, never a global Git prune. Inventory caps are 100000 workspace entries/256MiB, 1024 registration entries/256MiB and 4096 runtime entries/128MiB, depth 128. OS filesystem calls remain subject to I/O latency; no hard syscall deadline is claimed. Failure retains uncertain resources and is separate from the domain draft. It returns an operational error, blocks authority/continuation and remains an error on idempotent replay, without another spawn. Inspect the recorded runtime identifier and registration rather than rerunning the adapter to compensate.\nPOSE.md:1025:Retain evidence without automatic expiry (`retention_ttl_seconds: 0`); newly written run/transcript files use mode 0600 and private sessions retain their existing 0700/0600 policy. Export retained redacted run/transcript, witness, attempt/continuation receipts and optional proof together, preserving inventory and digests; exclude private sessions and issuer keys. Raw-byte digests precede redaction; retained-byte digests, canonical run identity, witness and native signature follow. No retention operation removes rejection history or restores quota. Legacy records remain readable with no fabricated safety observations. No instance policy, sandbox backend or real vendor is activated by these examples.\nPOSE.md:1039:Pass `{session}` as an entire argv element, never inside shell code. Captures from both output channels must agree. Session references are saved under `.pose/review-private/` (directory 0700/files 0600, self-ignored by Git); keep that runtime material private. Public records contain the session digest. The supervisor redacts the captured reference from both transcript and draft before publishing; ambiguous captures withhold the unredacted bytes. Losing private material makes a run not recoverable; no replacement session is invented.\nPOSE.md:1041:Run `pose delegate ls [--json]` to inspect interruptions, whether a partial draft exists, reset instants and remaining wait. `pose doctor` warns about interrupted attempt endpoints. Runs finish completed, failed or interrupted; legacy stale records remain readable. Undeclared interruptions are unknown and not resumable. An interrupted draft is partial evidence, never a rejection or a review.\nPOSE.md:1068:Preview `pose review run-sign <run-id> --issuer <name> [--ttl <duration>]`, then add `--apply` to publish an immutable proof under `.pose/review-run-proofs/`. TTL is optional (no expiry when absent), positive when supplied and at most 365 days. Verify it with `pose review run-proof <run-id> <proof-id> [--json]`. The proof signs the captured executor declaration; it is not an independent vendor receipt or human confirmation. Failed or interrupted audit records disclose their state and `complete_review=false`. Unknown or ambiguous draft decisions also keep that flag false. A true flag describes completed delivery with one draft decision, not approval or complete typed-conclusion validation. A completed run with incomplete stdin or truncated output cannot establish a completed review. Legacy runs have no captured provenance and cannot be upgraded by signing today's configuration. `pose review run-proof` and the issuer lookups of the verdict readers ask the question the closeout asks: for a run whose scope is done, the proof as it was given (the record stands, the issuer still holds its pin; routine drift of the repository and the proof's expiry are not asked, because the expiry bounds when a proof may back a new attestation, not whether an attestation already given to a done scope stands); for an open scope, as the repository is now.\nPOSE.md:1072:**Conclusions of delegated reviews.** `pose review conclude <run> [--issuer <name>] [--apply] [--json]` records the conclusion of a completed review run: the run's final message carries its typed conclusion in the pose-draft block — `criteria` (`id`, `disposition` passed|finding|not-applicable, `evidence` as `<class>:<ref>`, `rationale`, and `mappings` where the plan asks for structural mappings) and `tools` (`id`, `component`, `disposition`, `evidence`, `rationale`), the brief of template `review-brief/7` (the text a template version renders is frozen by a golden digest, so a change to it takes a new version) asks for them and states the rules the engine checks before it records one (a judgment criterion passed carries a rationale, its evidence is a ref the bundle sealed, a finding criterion names a finding of the draft, structural facts are answered as `mappings`; a draft that breaks one is refused whole; a round after the first also names the findings it accounts for by the identifiers the brief lists, with their round, never a bare F1) — and the engine signs the run's proof and the attestation with the native issuer, `--issuer` or `conclusions.issuer` in `.pose/policy/delegation.json` (the key stays outside the project), and records it through the same domain writer and preflight as `pose review attest --run --run-proof --sign`. A follow-up finding with `action`, `owner` and `review_by` becomes an accepted risk when its severity is one the sealed review policy accepts (low by default); any other finding stays open. A rejecting conclusion is recorded too and blocks the scope until the bundle changes. A run that is not a completed review, is superseded, captured no provenance, has an incomplete cleanup or record, carries no valid draft for its round, or whose bundle no longer governs, records no attestation: its conclusion record, in the `review-conclusions` directory of `.pose`, keeps the reasons. A run concludes once: a lock the system releases when the process ends is held from the first read to the last write, concluding it again returns its record, and a reservation an interrupted conclusion left is completed from the attestation it recorded only if that attestation verifies as the run's conclusion, otherwise the run is concluded in its place. Whoever signs it, an attestation of a run briefed with `review-brief/3` to `/7` states that run's conclusion: criteria, tools or findings its draft did not declare are refused. With review policy `delegated_review: true` and `conclusions.issuer` set, `pose delegate review` records the conclusion the moment the run completes; otherwise nothing is automatic. An adjudication or a smoke run concludes the same way, under the same checks, lock and refusals, and never as an approval. The brief of template `adjudication-brief/2` lists the questions the scope's spec records — each decision `D<n>` and requirement `R<n>` — and the pose-draft block answers each in `questions` (`id`, `verdict` holds|does-not-hold|cannot-determine, `evidence`, `rationale`); the verdict is recorded as an adjudication record of the spec in the `adjudications` directory of `.pose`, and `lint-spec` fails, and `close` refuses, on a question the current bundle's adjudication says does not hold and warns on one it cannot determine or on a record that does not verify. The brief of template `smoke-brief/2` asks for `surfaces` (`ref`, `outcome` observed|not-observed|not-reachable, `commands`, `observed`), one per delivery target; the observations are recorded as validation results of evidence class `smoke`, one required check per target that passes when it was observed and is evidence of that target of that spec only, its head, time and provenance taken from the run and the current index, in the `results/smoke` directory of `.pose`, which `surface-check` reads beside the results path, so a delivery profile may demand `smoke`. A verdict that does not answer exactly what its brief asked records nothing; every record is verified on read — its run's proof verifies and it is what the run's draft concludes — and one that does not yields nothing. Runs briefed with `adjudication-brief/1` and `smoke-brief/1` keep verifying with their template.\nPOSE.md:1076:Opt in to the verification bar with `delegated_review: true` in review policy only when preparing adoption. This live bar requires a signed run reference for agent different-actor review and refuses a prefix-only identity; it does not enable or add the future catalog capability. Non-adopting instances keep declared behavior. Under explicit executor-attested trust, normalized vendor OR model difference satisfies different-actor (vendor difference preferred); unknown baselines and the same vendor/model refuse. A machine run never satisfies mandatory-human. Catalog adoption, cutoffs/rollback and automatic conclusion application remain separate capability work.\nPOSE.md:1078:Read the same current assurance through verify, review-check, MCP and `pose state --attention`: `machine-attested` names the signed executor declaration, its source, run/proof and vendor/model comparisons. Current issuer pins, project/audience/time, sealed inputs, retained artifacts and original/continuation receipts are reverified. Revocation, missing receipts, rewritten data or stale inputs remove machine assurance. Signing does not reset quotas or erase earlier rejecting runs. The origin worktree is recorded with the run, not required of the reader: a proof verifies in any checkout of the project, so a run a CI job or the Conductor performed is verified like a local one; no universal containment is claimed.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_30","type":"agent_message","text":"The reviewed paths route aggregate decisions through the shared gate. I found protections for `always`, recorded rejections, unknown inputs, and interrupted closes. I’m checking the remaining parser edges and projection callers; the focused tests and two required gates have not returned yet."}}
